window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-biotech-principles","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes · Practice","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Disarmed Ti plasmid\n(B) Disarmed retrovirus\n(C) β-galactosidase coding sequence\n(D) Chromogenic substrate\nList-II\n(I) Vector used to deliver desirable genes into animal cells\n(II) Gives blue colour in colonies whose plasmid has no insert\n(III) Vector used to deliver genes of interest into a variety of plants\n(IV) Site where the insertion of foreign DNA causes insertional inactivation\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The disarmed Ti plasmid of A. tumefaciens delivers genes into a variety of plants: A-III.\n(B) A disarmed retrovirus delivers desirable genes into animal cells: B-I.\n(C) Foreign DNA inserted into the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence causes insertional inactivation: C-IV.\n(D) The chromogenic substrate turns blue where the enzyme is intact, i.e. in colonies whose plasmid has no insert: D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The primers used in a polymerase chain reaction are best described as:","image":"","options":["Small chemically synthesised oligonucleotides complementary to regions of the target DNA","Restriction enzymes that cut the DNA at palindromic sites","Long RNA molecules isolated from the host cell","Plasmids that carry the gene of interest into the host"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: PCR primers are small, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides. They are complementary to the regions flanking the target DNA.\nDNA polymerase cannot start a new strand on its own. The primer supplies the starting point that the enzyme extends.\nWhy not (2): restriction enzymes cut DNA, whereas primers are nucleic acid, not enzymes.\nWhy not (3) and (4): primers are short and synthetic, not long RNAs taken from a cell. They are also not vectors like plasmids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the first recombinant DNA experiment, which enzyme joined the cut antibiotic resistance gene to the plasmid vector?","image":"","options":["Restriction endonuclease","DNA polymerase","Exonuclease","DNA ligase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Restriction enzymes acted as 'molecular scissors' to cut the DNA in the first experiment.\nDNA ligase then joined the cut ends of the antibiotic resistance gene and the plasmid, forming the recombinant plasmid.\n(1) fails: restriction endonuclease cuts DNA but does not join it.\n(2) fails: DNA polymerase synthesises DNA.\n(3) fails: an exonuclease removes nucleotides from the ends of DNA.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A DNA segment contains the EcoRI site shown below:\n5' —— GAATTC —— 3'\n3' —— CTTAAG —— 5'\nEcoRI cuts each strand between G and A. Which option correctly describes the ends of the two fragments formed?","image":"","options":["Each fragment carries a single-stranded 5'-AATT-3' overhang, and the two overhangs are complementary to each other","Both fragments have ends with no overhang, so ligase cannot use base pairing to align them","Each fragment carries a two-nucleotide 5'-AT-3' overhang","Each fragment carries a five-nucleotide 5'-CTTAA-3' overhang"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The top strand is cut G|AATTC and the bottom strand is cut CTTAA|G.\nAfter the cut, the bases AATT stay unpaired on each fragment. Read 5' → 3', each overhang is 5'-AATT-3'.\nAATT is self-complementary, so the two sticky ends can hydrogen bond with each other or with any EcoRI-cut vector.\n(3) is wrong because the overhang has four nucleotides, not two.\n(4) is wrong because it misreads the overhang and gets both its length and sequence wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Of the two enzymes isolated from Escherichia coli in 1963 that restricted the growth of bacteriophage, only one was named restriction endonuclease.\nReason (R): One of these enzymes added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In 1963, two E. coli enzymes that restricted phage growth were isolated, but only one was named restriction endonuclease.\nR is true. One enzyme added methyl groups to DNA, and the other cut DNA.\nOnly the enzyme that cut DNA got the name restriction endonuclease. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the chromogenic selection method, foreign DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene of a vector. On a medium containing a chromogenic substrate, how do bacterial colonies carrying recombinant plasmids appear?","image":"","options":["Blue","Colourless","Bright orange","Red"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When foreign DNA is inserted into the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence, the gene is inactivated. This is called insertional inactivation.\nWithout the enzyme, the chromogenic substrate is not converted into a blue product, so recombinant colonies stay colourless.\nBlue colonies (option 1) are tempting, but they contain plasmids without an insert, where the enzyme is still active.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Besides being the point where replication starts, which other role does the origin of replication (ori) of a cloning vector play?","image":"","options":["It allows the host cell to be selected on antibiotic medium","It controls the copy number of the linked DNA","It provides the recognition site for restriction enzymes","It makes the host cell competent to take up DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Replication starts at the ori, and the ori also controls the copy number of any DNA linked to it.\nSo to get many copies of a target DNA, it is cloned in a vector whose ori supports a high copy number.\nOption (1) fails because selection on antibiotic medium depends on selectable markers such as ampR, not on ori.\nOptions (3) and (4) also fail: restriction sites are separate sequences, and competence comes from treating cells with divalent cations such as $Ca^{2+}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) DNA cannot pass through cell membranes because it is hydrophilic.\n(b) Competent bacterial cells are prepared using a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as calcium.\n(c) During heat shock, cells are placed briefly at 42°C.\n(d) The gene gun uses DNA-coated microparticles of silver or platinum.\n(e) Disarmed retroviruses are used to deliver desirable genes into animal cells.\n(f) Micro-injection delivers recombinant DNA into the cytoplasm of plant cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: DNA is hydrophilic, so it cannot cross the lipid membrane.\n(b) Correct: a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as Ca$^{2+}$ makes cells competent.\n(c) Correct: heat shock is a brief exposure to 42°C.\n(d) Incorrect: the gene gun uses gold or tungsten particles, not silver or platinum.\n(e) Correct: disarmed retroviruses deliver desirable genes into animal cells.\n(f) Incorrect: micro-injection delivers DNA into the nucleus of animal cells, not the cytoplasm of plant cells.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a protein-encoding gene is expressed in a heterologous host, the protein produced is called a:","image":"","options":["Recombinant protein","Native protein","Chimeric enzyme","Reporter protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A heterologous host is an organism of a different species from the one the gene came from.\nNCERT calls a protein made by expressing a foreign gene in such a host a recombinant protein.\nA native protein is made by the organism's own genes, so option (2) does not fit.\nChimeric enzyme and reporter protein describe other ideas.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, biotechnology deals with techniques that use:","image":"","options":["Live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans","Only synthetic chemical reactions to manufacture drugs","Only physical instruments to observe living cells","Only genetically modified animals for laboratory research"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines biotechnology as techniques that use live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans.\nOption (1) states this definition directly.\nOption (2) uses no biological agent. Option (3) involves only observation, not production. Option (4) is too narrow, because microbes, plants and enzymes are also used.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Foreign DNA is ligated at the BamH I site of the vector pBR322, and E. coli cells are transformed. Four colonies were tested for growth on two media:\nColony P: grows on ampicillin, grows on tetracycline\nColony Q: grows on ampicillin, does not grow on tetracycline\nColony R: does not grow on ampicillin, grows on tetracycline\nColony S: does not grow on either\nWhich colony carries the recombinant plasmid?","image":"","options":["Colony P","Colony Q","Colony R","Colony S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In pBR322 the BamH I site lies inside the tetracycline resistance gene ($tet^R$). The ampicillin resistance gene ($amp^R$) is left intact.\nInserting foreign DNA at this site inactivates $tet^R$ (insertional inactivation). Recombinant cells therefore grow on ampicillin but not on tetracycline. That is colony Q.\nColony P grows on both drugs, so both genes work. It carries a non-recombinant plasmid.\nColony S grows on neither, so it was not transformed.\nColony R's pattern ($amp$-sensitive, $tet$-resistant) cannot come from a BamH I insert.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bacteriophages have very high copy numbers of their genome within bacterial cells because of their high number per cell.\nStatement II: All plasmids are present in at least 15–100 copies per bacterial cell.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT notes that phages are present in high numbers per cell, so their genome reaches very high copy numbers inside the bacterium.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Plasmid copy number varies. Some plasmids have only one or two copies per cell, while others have 15–100 or more. The word 'All' makes the statement false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens is used as a cloning vector to transfer genes of interest into a variety of plants.\nReason (R): The Ti plasmid has been modified so that it is no longer pathogenic to plants but can still use its mechanism to deliver genes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens is now used as a cloning vector to deliver genes of interest into many plants.\nR is true. The plasmid has been disarmed: its tumour-causing ability is removed, but it can still use its natural mechanism to transfer DNA into plant cells.\nR directly explains A, because keeping the delivery mechanism without the disease is what makes the plasmid usable as a vector.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about making a bacterial host competent for transformation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Bacterial cells are treated with a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as calcium","Cells mixed with recombinant DNA are kept on ice, given a brief heat shock at $42^{\\circ}C$, and then returned to ice","DNA is a hydrophobic molecule and therefore cannot pass through cell membranes on its own","Calcium treatment increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: cells are treated with a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as $Ca^{2+}$.\n(2) Correct: cells are kept on ice, given a heat shock at $42^{\\circ}C$, and returned to ice.\n(3) Incorrect: DNA cannot cross membranes because it is hydrophilic, not hydrophobic.\n(4) Correct: calcium treatment improves DNA entry through pores in the cell wall.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (method of introducing alien DNA) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(A) Micro-injection\n(B) Biolistics\n(C) Disarmed pathogen vector\n(D) Divalent cation treatment followed by heat shock\nList-II:\n(I) Bacterial cells are made competent and then briefly placed at 42°C\n(II) Recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell\n(III) Plant cells are bombarded with DNA-coated micro-particles\n(IV) The vector infects the cell and transfers the recombinant DNA into the host\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Micro-injection: recombinant DNA is injected directly into the nucleus of an animal cell → II.\n(B) Biolistics: plant cells are bombarded with DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles → III.\n(C) Disarmed pathogen vector (e.g., Agrobacterium): it infects the cell and transfers the recombinant DNA → IV.\n(D) Divalent cation (Ca$^{2+}$) treatment, then heat shock at 42°C: bacterial cells become competent → I.\nSo the match is A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In 1963, two enzymes that restricted the growth of bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated. One of them added methyl groups to DNA. What was the other enzyme, which cut DNA, called?","image":"","options":["DNA ligase","Restriction endonuclease","Exonuclease","DNA polymerase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: in 1963, two enzymes that restrict bacteriophage growth in E. coli were isolated.\nOne enzyme added methyl groups to DNA. The other cut DNA and was named restriction endonuclease.\nDNA ligase is the tempting option, but it joins DNA fragments rather than cutting them.\nExonuclease and DNA polymerase were not the phage-restricting enzymes described.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Making curd, bread and wine are microbe-mediated processes that can be regarded as forms of biotechnology in the broad sense.\n(b) In its restricted present-day sense, biotechnology refers to processes that use genetically modified organisms to achieve such products on a larger scale.\n(c) Bioprocess engineering requires a sterile ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities.\n(d) Asexual reproduction permits variation, whereas sexual reproduction preserves the genetic information.\n(e) The definition of biotechnology given by the European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB) covers only modern molecular biotechnology and excludes the traditional view.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: making curd, bread and wine are microbe-mediated processes, so they count as biotechnology in the broad sense.\n(b) Correct: in the restricted modern sense, biotechnology uses genetically modified organisms to make such products on a larger scale.\n(c) Correct: a sterile ambience lets only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grow in large quantity.\n(d) Incorrect: the roles are reversed. Sexual reproduction permits variation, while asexual reproduction preserves genetic information.\n(e) Incorrect: the EFB definition covers both the traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A good cloning vector should have very few, preferably a single, recognition site for a commonly used restriction enzyme. The main reason is that:","image":"","options":["A single site prevents the host from degrading the vector","Several sites would cut the vector into many fragments and complicate gene cloning","Restriction enzymes cannot act on DNA that has more than one recognition site","A single site increases the copy number of the vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A good vector needs a single (or very few) recognition site for a given restriction enzyme.\nIf there are several sites, the enzyme cuts the vector into many fragments, which complicates gene cloning. Option (2) is correct.\nOption (4) fails because copy number is controlled by ori, not by cloning sites.\nOption (3) fails because restriction enzymes can cut DNA at multiple sites.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A student cuts the source DNA containing the gene of interest with EcoRI and cuts the plasmid vector with BamHI. She then mixes the two and adds DNA ligase. What is the most likely outcome, according to the principle described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["A recombinant plasmid forms efficiently, because ligase joins any two DNA ends","A recombinant plasmid will normally not form, because the two DNAs were not cut with the same restriction enzyme and so lack matching sticky ends","A recombinant plasmid forms, but only after ribonuclease treatment","The gene of interest is amplified a billion times inside the tube"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT principle: the source DNA and the vector must be cut with the same restriction enzyme. This gives them complementary sticky ends that can pair.\nEcoRI and BamHI leave different overhangs, so the ends cannot base-pair.\nDNA ligase only seals ends that have already paired. It does not create complementarity, so option (1) is wrong.\nRibonuclease removes RNA, and amplification happens only in PCR, so options (3) and (4) are irrelevant.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction digest gives four DNA fragments of 500 bp, 1500 bp, 3000 bp and 8000 bp. They are separated by agarose gel electrophoresis. Which fragment's band will lie closest to the well where the sample was loaded?","image":"","options":["500 bp","1500 bp","3000 bp","8000 bp"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"DNA is negatively charged, so in agarose gel electrophoresis it moves towards the anode.\nThe agarose matrix acts as a sieve, separating fragments by size. Smaller fragments move farther from the well.\nThe largest fragment, 8000 bp, moves the least, so its band lies closest to the well.\nThe 500 bp fragment, which a student might be tempted to pick, actually moves the farthest.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Restriction enzymes are classified as endonucleases.\nReason (R): Restriction enzymes remove nucleotides one by one from the free ends of a DNA molecule.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: restriction enzymes belong to the class of endonucleases.\nEndonucleases cut at specific positions within the DNA, at the recognition sequence.\nR is false: removing nucleotides one by one from the free ends is what exonucleases do, not restriction enzymes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign DNA fragment is ligated at the BamH I site of the vector pBR322, and the transformed E. coli cells are plated. Which of the following correctly describes the growth of cells carrying the recombinant plasmid?","image":"","options":["They grow on both ampicillin and tetracycline media","They grow on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium","They grow on tetracycline medium but not on ampicillin medium","They grow on neither ampicillin nor tetracycline medium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"pBR322 carries two antibiotic resistance genes: $amp^R$ and $tet^R$.\nThe BamH I recognition site lies inside the $tet^R$ gene. Ligating foreign DNA there disrupts the gene. This is called insertional inactivation.\nRecombinant cells therefore lose tetracycline resistance but keep ampicillin resistance. They grow on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium.\nNon-recombinant cells have both genes intact, so they grow on both media. That is option (1), the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When E. coli cells exposed to a plasmid carrying the ampicillin resistance gene are spread on agar containing ampicillin, untransformed cells die.\nReason (R): Normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against ampicillin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: on ampicillin agar, only transformed cells survive, and untransformed cells die.\nR is true: normal E. coli has no resistance to ampicillin.\nR explains A. Untransformed cells lack the ampR gene, so ampicillin kills them. Cells that took up the plasmid express ampR and survive.\nThis is exactly why ampR works as a selectable marker.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Bioprocess engineering, one of the two core techniques behind modern biotechnology, mainly involves:","image":"","options":["Altering the chemistry of DNA and RNA to change the phenotype of a host","Maintaining a sterile, contamination-free ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities","Crossing two plant varieties to combine their desirable traits","Separating DNA fragments according to their size on an agarose gel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Modern biotechnology rests on two core techniques: genetic engineering and bioprocess engineering.\nBioprocess engineering keeps a sterile, contamination-free ambience in chemical engineering processes. Only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell then grows in large quantities to make antibiotics, vaccines and enzymes.\nOption (1) is the tempting wrong choice. Altering the chemistry of DNA or RNA to change the host phenotype describes genetic engineering.\nCrossing varieties is conventional breeding, and gel separation is only an analytical tool.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material to be removed or broken during DNA isolation) with List-II (enzyme used).\nList-I:\nA. Bacterial cell wall\nB. Fungal cell wall\nC. Histone proteins\nD. Plant cell wall\nList-II:\nI. Protease\nII. Cellulase\nIII. Lysozyme\nIV. Chitinase","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. The bacterial cell wall is broken by lysozyme, so A-III.\nB. The fungal cell wall is made of chitin and is broken by chitinase, so B-IV.\nC. Histone proteins are digested by protease, so C-I.\nD. The plant cell wall is made of cellulose and is broken by cellulase, so D-II.\nOption (2) is the trap: it swaps the enzymes for bacteria and fungi.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the chromogenic substrate method, blue colonies are identified as recombinant colonies.\nStatement II: Insertion of foreign DNA within the coding sequence of $\\beta$-galactosidase inactivates the gene for this enzyme.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. Inserting foreign DNA within the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence inactivates the gene (insertional inactivation).\nStatement I is incorrect. Recombinant colonies lack an active enzyme, so they cannot convert the chromogenic substrate and stay colourless. Blue colonies have an intact gene, so they are non-recombinants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding large-scale production of a recombinant product is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The separation and purification steps carried out after the biosynthetic stage are collectively called downstream processing.","In a continuous culture, spent medium is drained from one side while fresh medium is added from the other, so that cells stay in the stationary phase, where biomass is maximum.","The downstream processing and quality control testing required differ from one product to another.","A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base, which helps in mixing the reactor contents."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: separation and purification after biosynthesis are together called downstream processing.\n(2) Incorrect: continuous culture keeps cells in the log (exponential) phase, which is their most active phase. That is why it gives more biomass. Cells are not held in the stationary phase.\n(3) Correct: downstream processing and quality control vary from product to product.\n(4) Correct: a stirred-tank reactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base, which aids mixing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a bacterial cell is lysed, RNA is released along with the DNA. Which treatment removes this RNA contamination?","image":"","options":["Treatment with protease","Treatment with lysozyme","Treatment with DNA ligase","Treatment with ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: during DNA isolation, RNA is removed by treating the lysate with ribonuclease, which digests RNA and leaves DNA intact.\nProtease removes proteins such as histones, not RNA.\nLysozyme breaks the bacterial cell wall and is used to lyse the cells in the first place.\nDNA ligase joins DNA fragments and does not remove anything.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments move farther from the well than larger fragments.\nReason (R): Agarose is a synthetic polymer that separates DNA fragments on the basis of their charge alone.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: DNA is negatively charged and moves toward the anode. Agarose gel acts as a sieve, so smaller fragments move farther from the well.\nR is false for two reasons:\n- Agarose is a natural polymer extracted from seaweeds, not a synthetic one.\n- Separation in the gel depends on fragment size, not on charge alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"After electrophoresis, a gel stained with ethidium bromide is exposed to UV light. How do the DNA fragments appear?","image":"","options":["As bright orange bands","As blue coloured bands","As dark black bands visible in daylight","As green fluorescent spots only at the wells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ethidium bromide binds to DNA. When the stained gel is exposed to UV radiation, the DNA fragments show up as bright orange bands.\nOption (2) is a trap: blue colour comes from the chromogenic test for β-galactosidase in recombinant screening, not from gel staining.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are methods for introducing recombinant DNA into host cells?\nMicro-injection, Biolistics, Elution, Calcium chloride treatment followed by heat shock, Spooling, Use of disarmed pathogen vectors, Gel electrophoresis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Methods that transfer DNA into host cells:\n(i) Micro-injection: DNA is injected directly into the nucleus of an animal cell.\n(ii) Biolistics: a gene gun fires DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles into plant cells.\n(iii) $CaCl_2$ treatment followed by heat shock: this makes bacterial cells competent to take up DNA.\n(iv) Disarmed pathogen vectors: for example, Agrobacterium or retroviruses carry the gene into the host.\nThe other three do not transfer DNA. Elution and spooling recover DNA, and gel electrophoresis separates fragments.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants.\n(b) T-DNA delivered by Agrobacterium transforms normal plant cells into tumour cells.\n(c) The tumour cells are directed to produce chemicals required by the pathogen.\n(d) The natural Ti plasmid is used as a plant vector without any modification.\n(e) Retroviruses in animals can transform normal cells into cancerous cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: A. tumefaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants.\n(b) Correct: the T-DNA it delivers transforms normal plant cells into tumour cells.\n(c) Correct: the tumour cells are made to produce chemicals the pathogen needs.\n(d) Incorrect: the Ti plasmid must first be disarmed so that it is non-pathogenic before it is used as a vector.\n(e) Correct: retroviruses can transform normal animal cells into cancerous cells.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease to be isolated and characterised whose functioning depended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence.\nStatement II: Hind II was isolated in 1963, together with the methylating enzyme from Escherichia coli.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease to be isolated and characterised whose functioning depended on a specific nucleotide sequence.\nStatement II is incorrect. Hind II was isolated five years after 1963, not along with the methylating enzyme in 1963.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A student cuts a plasmid vector with EcoRI, which leaves AATT overhangs. He cuts the source DNA containing his gene with BamHI, which recognises 5'—GGATCC—3', cuts between G and G, and leaves GATC overhangs. He mixes the two and adds DNA ligase. The most likely outcome is that:","image":"","options":["the gene is efficiently inserted, because both enzymes recognise six-base palindromes","the gene is efficiently inserted, because ligase joins any two DNA ends with equal ease","the recombinant vector is not formed efficiently, because the AATT and GATC overhangs are not complementary and cannot base pair","the BamHI fragment is converted into an EcoRI fragment by ligase before joining"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sticky ends can join only if their single-stranded overhangs are complementary and base pair.\nThe EcoRI overhang (AATT) and the BamHI overhang (GATC) are not complementary, so the gene and the vector cannot anneal.\nWithout this pairing, ligase cannot join them efficiently, and no recombinant vector forms.\nBoth sites being palindromes does not make their overhangs match, so option (1) is wrong.\nThis is why the vector and source DNA are cut with the same enzyme.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about introducing recombinant DNA into host cells are correct?\n(i) DNA is hydrophilic and therefore cannot pass through cell membranes on its own.\n(ii) Bacterial cells are made competent by treating them with a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as calcium.\n(iii) During transformation, cells with recombinant DNA are kept on ice, given a brief heat shock at 42°C, and then returned to ice.\n(iv) In micro-injection, recombinant DNA is injected directly into the nucleus of a plant cell.\n(v) In biolistics, cells are bombarded with high-velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: DNA is hydrophilic, so it cannot cross cell membranes on its own.\n(ii) Correct: treating bacteria with a divalent cation such as $Ca^{2+}$ makes them competent to take up DNA.\n(iii) Correct: cells are kept on ice, given a brief heat shock at 42°C, and then returned to ice.\n(iv) Incorrect: micro-injection delivers recombinant DNA into the nucleus of an animal cell, not a plant cell.\n(v) Correct: in biolistics (gene gun), cells are bombarded with DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A vector carries an ampicillin resistance gene and a $\\beta$-galactosidase gene. Foreign DNA is ligated at a restriction site inside the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, and E. coli cells are transformed. The cells are spread on a single plate containing both ampicillin and a chromogenic substrate. Which colonies should be picked as recombinants?","image":"","options":["Blue colonies growing on the plate","Colourless colonies growing on the plate","Cells that fail to grow on the plate","All colonies on the plate, since every transformant carries the insert"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ampicillin kills untransformed E. coli, so every colony that grows on the plate carries the vector.\nIf the insert sits inside the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, the gene is inactivated. These cells cannot act on the chromogenic substrate and form colourless colonies, so they are the recombinants.\nVector without an insert keeps an active enzyme and gives blue colonies, so option (1) is wrong.\nOption (4) fails because not every transformant carries the insert. Option (3) fails because cells that do not grow are untransformed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are carried out on a biotechnological product after completion of the biosynthetic stage and before it is marketed?\nSeparation; Purification; Formulation with suitable preservatives; Clinical trials (for drugs); Quality control testing; Heat shock of competent cells; Primer annealing","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Separation: done after biosynthesis (downstream processing).\nPurification: done after biosynthesis (downstream processing).\nFormulation with suitable preservatives: done after biosynthesis.\nClinical trials for drugs: done after formulation.\nQuality control testing: done after biosynthesis.\nHeat shock of competent cells: no. It is part of transformation, which happens before biosynthesis.\nPrimer annealing: no. It is a PCR step used during gene amplification.\nTotal = 5 steps.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a protein-encoding gene from one organism is expressed in a different (heterologous) host organism, the protein produced is called a:","image":"","options":["Recombinant protein","Restriction protein","Reporter protein","Chimeric enzyme"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): a protein is called a recombinant protein when its encoding gene is expressed in a heterologous host.\nOption (1) therefore fits.\nOption (3) is the tempting wrong choice. Reporter enzymes or proteins are markers used only to monitor transformation.\n'Restriction protein' and 'chimeric enzyme' are not NCERT terms for such a product.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"An agarose gel has four lanes. Lane 1 contains undigested linear DNA, and lanes 2 to 4 contain the same DNA digested with restriction enzymes. The gel is stained and viewed. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The samples were loaded in wells at the cathode (negative) end of the gel.\n(ii) The undigested linear DNA in lane 1 travels farther than any of its own restriction fragments.\n(iii) The bands can be seen in visible light even without staining.\n(iv) Cutting a band out of the gel and extracting DNA from the gel piece is called elution.\n(v) In a digested lane, the band nearest the well represents the largest fragment.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: DNA is negatively charged, so it is loaded at the cathode end and moves towards the anode.\n(ii) Incorrect: intact DNA is larger than any of its fragments, so it moves less far through the agarose sieve.\n(iii) Incorrect: DNA is invisible until it is stained with ethidium bromide and exposed to UV light, when it shows orange bands.\n(iv) Correct: cutting out a band and extracting the DNA from the gel piece is called elution.\n(v) Correct: the largest fragment moves least, so it stays nearest the well.\nThree statements, (i), (iv) and (v), are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following double-stranded sequences (only one strand shown, 5' → 3') are palindromic?\nGAATTC, GGATCC, AAGCTT, GACTTC, GATATC, AGCTTA","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Test: a sequence is palindromic if its reverse complement is identical to it.\nGAATTC → GAATTC ✓.\nGGATCC → GGATCC ✓.\nAAGCTT → AAGCTT ✓.\nGACTTC → GAAGTC ✗.\nGATATC → GATATC ✓.\nAGCTTA → TAAGCT ✗.\nFour sequences are palindromic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A mosquito acts as an insect vector that carries the malarial parasite into the human body. In the same way, a plasmid serves as a vector to carry foreign DNA into a host. Which description of a plasmid matches NCERT?","image":"","options":["Autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA","Linear DNA segment that replicates only after integrating into the chromosome","Circular RNA molecule found in bacterial cytoplasm","Protein coat that encloses a viral genome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines plasmids as autonomously replicating, circular, extra-chromosomal DNA.\nBecause a plasmid replicates independently of the chromosome, any DNA linked to it is also copied. This is what makes it useful as a vector.\n(2) fails: plasmids are circular and do not need to integrate into the chromosome to replicate.\n(3) fails: plasmids are made of DNA, not RNA.\n(4) fails: a protein coat around a genome describes a viral capsid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Each sequence below is one strand of a double-stranded DNA, written $5' \\rightarrow 3'$. How many of them are palindromic in the sense used for restriction sites?\n5'-GAATTC-3'\n5'-GGATCC-3'\n5'-GATATC-3'\n5'-GTACTC-3'\n5'-AACGTT-3'\n5'-CAGCTC-3'","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands in the $5' \\rightarrow 3'$ direction, so the sequence must equal its reverse complement. This holds for GAATTC, GGATCC, GATATC and AACGTT, giving 4. GTACTC (reverse complement GAGTAC) and CAGCTC (reverse complement GAGCTG) fail the test.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Foreign DNA is ligated at the Sal I site, which lies within tetR, of pBR322. Transformed E. coli cells are spread on an ampicillin plate, and the colonies are then transferred to a tetracycline plate. Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Colonies that grow on the ampicillin plate but are absent from the tetracycline plate carry the recombinant plasmid.\nStatement II: Selection based on inactivation of an antibiotic resistance gene is considered cumbersome because it requires plating on two plates with different antibiotics.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. An insert at Sal I inactivates $tet^R$, so recombinants grow on ampicillin but are missing from the tetracycline replica plate.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT calls this method cumbersome because it needs simultaneous plating on two plates with different antibiotics. This is why chromogenic ($\\beta$-galactosidase) selection was developed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"EcoRI cuts its site 5'—GAATTC—3' between G and A on each strand. The ends of the resulting fragments will be:","image":"","options":["blunt ends with no single-stranded region","two-base single-stranded overhangs of AT","four-base single-stranded overhangs reading AATT (5' → 3') on each fragment","six-base single-stranded overhangs reading GAATTC"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The site is 5'—G↓AATTC—3' on the top strand and 3'—CTTAA↑G—5' on the bottom strand.\nCutting between G and A on both strands leaves AATT unpaired on each fragment, as a 5' overhang reading AATT (5' → 3').\nThe cut lies away from the centre of the palindrome, so the ends are sticky, not blunt.\nThe overhang is 4 bases long, not 2 or 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In each PCR cycle shown in Figure 9.6, there is a step in which short, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides bind to complementary regions on the single-stranded template DNA. What is this step called?","image":"","options":["Denaturation","Primer annealing","Extension","Ligation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each PCR cycle has three steps: denaturation, annealing and extension.\nDenaturation uses heat to separate the two DNA strands.\nAnnealing is when two sets of short synthetic primers bind to complementary sequences on the single strands. This is the step described in the question.\nExtension is when DNA polymerase lengthens the bound primers.\nLigation is not a step of PCR.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"During DNA isolation, purified DNA comes out of the suspension as a collection of fine threads that can be removed by spooling. What causes the DNA to separate out in this way?","image":"","options":["Addition of protease","Addition of chilled ethanol","Exposure to UV light after staining with ethidium bromide","Heat shock at 42°C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: after RNA and proteins are removed, chilled ethanol is added. Purified DNA precipitates as a mass of fine threads that can be spooled out.\nProtease only removes proteins and does not precipitate DNA.\nEthidium bromide with UV light is used to see DNA bands in a gel.\nHeat shock at 42°C is part of transformation, not DNA isolation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can be regarded as part of biotechnology (in its broad sense)?\nMaking curd from milk\nProducing wine by fermentation\nIn vitro fertilisation leading to a test-tube baby\nDeveloping a DNA vaccine\nCorrecting a defective gene\nSmelting iron ore in a blast furnace","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Making curd: yes, it uses Lactobacillus.\nProducing wine: yes, it uses yeast fermentation.\nIVF leading to a test-tube baby: yes, NCERT lists it as part of biotechnology.\nDeveloping a DNA vaccine: yes.\nCorrecting a defective gene (gene therapy): yes.\nSmelting iron ore: no, it is purely metallurgical and involves no organism, cell or enzyme.\nSo 5 of the 6 items count as biotechnology.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The ampicillin resistance gene on a vector is called a selectable marker because, in the presence of ampicillin, it allows transformed E. coli to grow while untransformed cells die.\nStatement II: Plating transformed cells on ampicillin alone is enough to separate cells carrying a recombinant plasmid from cells carrying a non-recombinant plasmid, when the insert lies outside the ampR gene.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In the presence of ampicillin, only cells carrying the plasmid (with ampR) survive. Untransformed cells die, which is why ampR is a selectable marker.\nStatement II is incorrect. If the insert lies outside ampR, both recombinant and non-recombinant plasmids keep an intact ampR gene, so both kinds of cells grow on ampicillin.\nA second screen is needed to pick out recombinants. Examples are insertional inactivation of tet$^R$ (replica plating on tetracycline) or of the β-galactosidase gene (blue-white colonies).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bioprocess engineering requires a sterile, contamination-free ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities.\nStatement II: Traditional hybridisation in plant and animal breeding allows only the desired genes to be introduced, without any undesirable genes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Bioprocess engineering keeps a sterile, contamination-free ambience so that only the desired microbe or eukaryotic cell grows in large quantities.\nStatement II is incorrect. Traditional hybridisation often brings in and multiplies undesirable genes along with the desired ones. Genetic engineering overcomes this by introducing only the desired genes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The thermostable DNA polymerase used in the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is isolated from which organism?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Thermus aquaticus","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Salmonella typhimurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"PCR uses Taq polymerase, a heat-stable DNA polymerase.\nIt is isolated from the thermophilic bacterium Thermus aquaticus, which lives in hot springs.\nE. coli is the usual cloning host. Its own DNA polymerase is destroyed at denaturation temperatures, so it cannot be the answer.\nAgrobacterium tumefaciens supplies the Ti plasmid vector, and Salmonella typhimurium was used in early cloning work.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following has been disarmed and is now used as a vector to deliver desirable genes into animal cells?","image":"","options":["Ti plasmid","Retroviruses","pBR322","Bacteriophage infecting E. coli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In animals, retroviruses can naturally turn normal cells into cancerous cells.\nOnce disarmed (made non-pathogenic), they are used as vectors to deliver desirable genes into animal cells.\nThe Ti plasmid is the tempting wrong option: it is the disarmed vector for plants, not animals.\npBR322 and bacteriophages are vectors for bacteria such as E. coli.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A piece of alien DNA transferred into an organism usually fails to multiply in the progeny cells unless it becomes part of a chromosome or is linked to a suitable sequence.\nReason (R): Replication of DNA is initiated only from a specific sequence called the origin of replication.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: alien DNA on its own usually cannot multiply in progeny cells. It must be integrated into a chromosome or linked to a suitable sequence.\nR is true: DNA replication starts only at a specific sequence, the origin of replication (ori).\nAlien DNA without an ori cannot replicate. Joining a chromosome or being linked to an ori supplies one, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a cloning vector, which sequence decides how many copies of the linked foreign DNA will be produced inside the host cell?","image":"","options":["Selectable marker gene","Origin of replication (ori)","Recognition site for a restriction enzyme","Sticky end of the inserted DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Replication starts at the origin of replication (ori). The ori also controls the copy number of any DNA linked to it.\nA vector with a high-copy ori is therefore chosen when many copies of the target DNA are needed.\nSelectable markers (option 1) only help identify transformants; they do not control copy number.\nRestriction sites and sticky ends only help in cutting and joining DNA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher wants to isolate DNA from leaf tissue. Which enzyme should be used first to break open the plant cells and release their contents?","image":"","options":["Lysozyme","Chitinase","Cellulase","Ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: plant cells are surrounded by a cell wall made mainly of cellulose. Cellulase digests this wall and releases the cell contents.\nLysozyme digests bacterial cell walls, and chitinase digests fungal cell walls. Neither suits leaf tissue.\nRibonuclease only removes RNA after the cells have already been lysed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which organisation gave the definition of biotechnology as 'the integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services'?","image":"","options":["World Health Organization (WHO)","European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB)","Genetic Engineering Approval Committee (GEAC)","Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The definition 'the integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services' was given by the European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB).\nIt is broad enough to include both traditional and modern molecular biotechnology.\nGEAC is the Indian committee that approves GM research and products. It regulates biotechnology but did not frame this definition.\nWHO and ICMR are health bodies and are not linked to this definition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction endonuclease recognises a palindromic sequence, one that reads the same on both strands when each is read in the $5' \\rightarrow 3'$ direction. Which of the following double-stranded sequences (top strand written $5' \\rightarrow 3'$) is a palindrome of this kind?","image":"","options":["$5'$-GAATTG-$3'$","$5'$-GGAATT-$3'$","$5'$-GGATCC-$3'$","$5'$-GCATGG-$3'$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands when each strand is read $5' \\rightarrow 3'$.\nFor GGATCC, the complementary strand is $3'$-CCTAGG-$5'$. Read $5' \\rightarrow 3'$, it is GGATCC, identical to the top strand. This is the BamH I site.\nThe other options fail. Their complementary strands, read $5' \\rightarrow 3'$, are CAATTC, AATTCC and CCATGC, which do not match the top strands.\nThe trap is to check only whether the top strand reads the same backwards. You must compare it with its complementary strand.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the three basic steps of genetically modifying an organism in the correct order?","image":"","options":["Introduction of DNA into host → identification of desirable genes → maintenance and transfer to progeny","Identification of DNA with desirable genes → introduction into host → maintenance in host and transfer to progeny","Identification of desirable genes → maintenance in host → introduction into host","Maintenance of DNA in host → identification of desirable genes → introduction into host"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three basic steps in genetically modifying an organism:\nStep 1: identify the DNA carrying the desirable genes.\nStep 2: introduce this DNA into the host.\nStep 3: maintain the introduced DNA in the host and transfer it to the progeny.\nOptions (3) and (4) put maintenance before introduction, which is impossible. Option (1) introduces DNA before the desirable genes have been identified.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following:\nRestriction enzymes, DNA ligase, polymerase enzymes, vectors, host organism, chitinase\nHow many of these are listed by NCERT as the key tools needed to accomplish recombinant DNA technology?","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the key tools of recombinant DNA technology as:\nrestriction enzymes, polymerase enzymes, ligases, vectors and the host organism.\nThat makes five key tools.\nChitinase is only used to break open fungal cells during DNA isolation, so it is not one of the key tools.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of a stirred-tank bioreactor) with List-II (its role).\nList-I:\n(A) Agitator (stirrer)\n(B) Sampling port\n(C) Sparger\n(D) Foam control system\nList-II:\n(I) Withdrawal of small volumes of culture periodically\n(II) Even mixing and oxygen availability throughout the vessel\n(III) Controlling froth formed during culture\n(IV) Bubbling sterile air into the culture\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Agitator (stirrer) mixes the contents evenly and keeps oxygen available throughout the vessel: A-II.\n(B) Sampling port lets small volumes of culture be withdrawn periodically: B-I.\n(C) Sparger bubbles sterile air into the culture: C-IV.\n(D) Foam control system controls the froth formed during culture: D-III.\nOption (2) is the tempting wrong choice because it swaps the roles of the agitator and the sparger.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): After transformation with pBR322, E. coli cells that have taken up the plasmid can be selected by plating on an ampicillin-containing medium.\nReason (R): Normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against ampicillin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: only E. coli cells carrying pBR322, with its ampR gene, survive on ampicillin medium.\nR is true: normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance to antibiotics such as ampicillin.\nBecause untransformed cells die on ampicillin, ampicillin resistance can work as a selectable marker. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A piece of alien DNA that gets integrated into the genome of the recipient may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.\nReason (R): After integration, the alien DNA becomes part of a chromosome, which carries an origin of replication that initiates replication.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: integrated alien DNA is copied along with the host DNA and passed to the progeny.\nR is true: once integrated, the alien DNA is part of a chromosome. The chromosome carries an origin of replication (ori), where replication starts.\nThe alien DNA is copied only because it now lies in a chromosome that has an ori, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the E. coli cloning vector pBR322, the gene labelled 'rop' codes for:","image":"","options":["Proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid","The enzyme that gives resistance to tetracycline","A restriction endonuclease that cuts the plasmid","The enzyme $\\beta$-galactosidase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the NCERT map of pBR322, the rop gene codes for proteins that take part in replication of the plasmid.\nTetracycline resistance comes from the separate tetR gene, so option (2) is wrong.\npBR322 carries no gene for a restriction enzyme or for $\\beta$-galactosidase. It only has recognition sites for restriction enzymes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Ethidium bromide\n(B) Chilled ethanol\n(C) Elution\n(D) Thermus aquaticus\nList-II\n(I) Source of thermostable DNA polymerase\n(II) Extracting separated DNA bands from the gel piece\n(III) Staining DNA so it can be seen under UV light\n(IV) Precipitating purified DNA\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Ethidium bromide stains DNA, which then shows as orange bands under UV light: A-III.\n(B) Chilled ethanol makes purified DNA precipitate out (fine threads): B-IV.\n(C) Elution means cutting out the separated DNA band from the agarose gel and extracting the DNA: C-II.\n(D) Thermus aquaticus is the source of the thermostable Taq DNA polymerase used in PCR: D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Making multiple copies of the antibiotic resistance gene in E. coli was called cloning of the antibiotic resistance gene in E. coli.\nStatement II: In E. coli, the recombinant plasmid replicated using a DNA polymerase carried over from Salmonella along with the plasmid.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: making many identical copies of a template DNA is called cloning. So multiplying the antibiotic resistance gene in E. coli was cloning that gene in E. coli.\nStatement II is incorrect: no DNA polymerase came over from Salmonella. The recombinant plasmid was replicated by the new host's own DNA polymerase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A cloning vector should preferably have only a single recognition site for a commonly used restriction enzyme.\nReason (R): More than one recognition site for the same enzyme in the vector will generate several fragments, which complicates gene cloning.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a vector should preferably have a single recognition site for a commonly used restriction enzyme.\nR is true: several sites would cut the vector into many fragments. The vector could then not simply be opened at one point and resealed with the insert.\nThis complication is exactly why single sites are preferred, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following double-stranded DNA sequences (top strand shown 5' → 3') is a palindrome that a restriction endonuclease could recognise?","image":"","options":["5'-GAATTG-3'","5'-AAGCTA-3'","5'-GGATCC-3'","5'-ACGCGA-3'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands when each is read 5'→3'. To test an option, take the complementary strand and read it 5'→3'.\nGGATCC: the complement read 5'→3' is GGATCC, the same sequence. It is the BamH I site.\nGAATTG gives CAATTC. AAGCTA gives TAGCTT. ACGCGA gives TCGCGT. None of these match the original.\nThe trap is looking for mirror-image letters on a single strand.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the restriction enzyme EcoRI, isolated from Escherichia coli RY 13, what does the letter 'R' represent?","image":"","options":["The genus of the bacterium","The species of the bacterium","The strain of the bacterium","The order in which the enzyme was isolated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Naming convention for restriction enzymes:\nThe first letter comes from the genus, here 'E' for Escherichia.\nThe next two letters come from the species, here 'co' for coli.\nThe next letter comes from the strain, here 'R' for RY 13.\nThe Roman numeral 'I' gives the order of isolation from that strain, so option (4) describes 'I', not 'R'.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The restriction enzyme BamH I was the first such enzyme isolated from strain H of the bacterium Bacillus amyloliquefaciens. Applying the standard naming convention, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The letter 'B' is derived from the genus Bacillus","The letters 'am' are derived from the species amyloliquefaciens","The letter 'H' indicates the strain of the bacterium","The Roman numeral 'I' indicates that the enzyme has only one recognition site in a vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: 'B' comes from the genus Bacillus.\n(2) Correct: 'am' comes from the species amyloliquefaciens.\n(3) Correct: 'H' indicates strain H.\n(4) Incorrect: the Roman numeral 'I' only shows the order of isolation, meaning this was the first enzyme from that strain. It says nothing about how many recognition sites the enzyme has in a vector.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the name of the restriction enzyme EcoRI, the letter 'R' indicates:","image":"","options":["The genus of the bacterium from which it was isolated","The strain of the bacterium from which it was isolated","That it is a restriction enzyme rather than a methylase","The order in which the enzyme was isolated from the bacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"EcoRI was isolated from Escherichia coli strain RY 13. Its name is built as follows:\n'E' comes from the genus (Escherichia).\n'co' comes from the species (coli).\n'R' comes from the strain (RY 13).\nThe Roman numeral 'I' gives the order in which the enzyme was isolated from that strain.\nThe common trap is reading 'R' as 'restriction'.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments travel farther from the well than larger fragments.\nReason (R): The agarose gel provides a sieving effect that resolves DNA fragments according to their size.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments are found farther from the wells than larger ones.\nR is true: the agarose matrix acts as a sieve and separates fragments by size.\nR explains A. Small fragments pass through the pores more easily, so they move farther in the same time. Large fragments are slowed by the matrix and stay nearer the wells.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Denaturation\n(b) Primer annealing\n(c) Extension\n(d) Primer\nList-II\n(i) DNA polymerase adds nucleotides using the template\n(ii) Short synthetic oligonucleotide\n(iii) High temperature separates the two DNA strands\n(iv) Oligonucleotides bind to complementary regions of single-stranded DNA\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Denaturation → (iii): high temperature separates the two DNA strands.\n(b) Primer annealing → (iv): oligonucleotides bind to complementary regions of the single strands.\n(c) Extension → (i): DNA polymerase adds nucleotides using the template.\n(d) Primer → (ii): a short synthetic oligonucleotide.\nCommon trap: option (1) swaps denaturation and annealing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (enzyme used during isolation of DNA) with List-II (its target/use):\nList-I\nA. Lysozyme\nB. Cellulase\nC. Chitinase\nD. Ribonuclease\nList-II\nI. Removal of RNA\nII. Breaking open fungal cells\nIII. Breaking open bacterial cells\nIV. Breaking open plant cells","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Match each enzyme to the polymer it digests:\nA. Lysozyme digests the bacterial cell wall → III.\nB. Cellulase digests cellulose in the plant cell wall → IV.\nC. Chitinase digests chitin in the fungal cell wall → II.\nD. Ribonuclease removes contaminating RNA → I.\nThe common trap is swapping cellulase and chitinase.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a method used to introduce recombinant DNA into plant cells, the cells are bombarded with high-velocity micro-particles coated with DNA. These micro-particles are made of:","image":"","options":["Gold or tungsten","Silver or copper","Calcium or magnesium","Iron or zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in biolistics (the gene gun), plant cells are bombarded with high-velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA.\nThese metals are dense and chemically inert, so they can carry DNA into cells without reacting.\nThe tempting option (3) is wrong: calcium is the divalent cation used to make bacterial cells competent. It is not a particle material.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about obtaining and processing a foreign gene product is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cloning a gene alone is not enough; the recombinant DNA must also be expressed under appropriate conditions to obtain the protein.","The stirrer in a stirred-tank bioreactor helps distribute oxygen evenly throughout the culture.","Downstream processing and quality control testing follow the same standard protocol for every biotechnological product.","A product meant to be used as a drug must undergo thorough clinical trials after formulation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Cloning alone is not enough; the recombinant DNA must also be expressed under appropriate conditions to give the protein.\n(2) Correct. The stirrer mixes the culture and keeps oxygen evenly available.\n(3) Incorrect. NCERT states that downstream processing and quality control testing vary from product to product. They do not follow one standard protocol.\n(4) Correct. A product meant for use as a drug must undergo thorough clinical trials after formulation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Restriction endonuclease\n(B) DNA ligase\n(C) Sticky end\n(D) Palindromic sequence\nList-II\n(I) Joins the ends of cut DNA molecules\n(II) Single-stranded overhang that hydrogen bonds with a complementary end\n(III) Base sequence that reads the same on both strands in the same orientation\n(IV) Recognises a specific sequence and cuts both strands in the sugar-phosphate backbone\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–IV: a restriction endonuclease recognises a specific sequence and cuts both strands in the sugar-phosphate backbone.\nB–I: DNA ligase joins the ends of cut DNA molecules.\nC–II: a sticky end is a single-stranded overhang that hydrogen bonds with a complementary end.\nD–III: a palindromic sequence reads the same on both strands in the same orientation.\nOption (2) is the trap: it swaps the cutting and joining enzymes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction digest produces four DNA fragments of 500 bp, 1500 bp, 3000 bp and 8000 bp. They are run together in one lane of an agarose gel. After staining with ethidium bromide and viewing under UV light, which fragment's band will lie closest to the wells where the sample was loaded?","image":"","options":["500 bp","1500 bp","3000 bp","8000 bp"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"DNA is negatively charged, so it moves towards the anode during agarose gel electrophoresis.\nThe agarose matrix acts as a sieve. Smaller fragments pass through more easily and travel farther.\nTherefore the largest fragment, 8000 bp, moves the least and its band lies closest to the wells.\nThe 500 bp fragment moves the farthest, so it is the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the biolistic (gene gun) method of gene transfer, cells are bombarded with high-velocity microparticles coated with DNA. These microparticles are made of:","image":"","options":["Silver or platinum","Gold or tungsten","Calcium or magnesium","Iron or copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: In biolistics (the gene gun), cells are bombarded with high-velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA.\nGold and tungsten are dense and chemically inert, which makes them suitable carriers.\nNCERT notes that this method is especially suitable for plants.\nSilver, platinum and the other metals listed are not the carriers NCERT names.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Agarose is the most commonly used matrix for separating DNA fragments by gel electrophoresis. What is agarose and where is it obtained from?","image":"","options":["A synthetic polymer made from acrylamide","A natural polymer extracted from seaweeds","A protein extracted from animal cartilage","A polysaccharide extracted from fungal cell walls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes agarose as a natural polymer extracted from seaweeds. Its gel acts as a sieve that separates DNA fragments by size.\nOption (1) describes polyacrylamide, a synthetic gel matrix.\nAgarose is not a protein, and it is not obtained from animal cartilage or fungal cell walls. That rules out options (3) and (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Bacterial cells must be specially treated before they take up plasmid DNA from their surroundings. According to NCERT, the basic reason DNA cannot pass through cell membranes on its own is that DNA is:","image":"","options":["A hydrophobic molecule","A hydrophilic molecule","A positively charged molecule","Too small to be recognised by membrane pores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: DNA is a hydrophilic molecule, so it cannot pass through the hydrophobic lipid bilayer of the cell membrane.\nThis is why bacterial cells must first be made 'competent' before they can take up plasmid DNA.\nOption (1) is the opposite of the truth. If DNA were hydrophobic, it could dissolve through the membrane.\nOption (3) is wrong because the phosphate backbone gives DNA a negative charge.\nOption (4) is wrong because DNA is a very large molecule, not a small one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about isolating and preparing DNA for recombinant DNA work are correct?\n(i) DNA must be free from other macromolecules before it can be cut with restriction enzymes.\n(ii) Breaking the cell releases DNA together with RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids.\n(iii) Chitinase is used to break open bacterial cells.\n(iv) Purified DNA precipitates when chilled ethanol is added.\n(v) The vector DNA is also cut with a restriction enzyme before ligation.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: DNA must be pure before restriction enzymes can cut it.\n(ii) Correct: Lysis releases DNA along with RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids.\n(iii) Incorrect: Chitinase acts on fungal walls. Lysozyme is used to open bacterial cells.\n(iv) Correct: Purified DNA precipitates as fine threads when chilled ethanol is added.\n(v) Correct: The vector is cut with the same restriction enzyme so that its ends match the insert.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A student makes E. coli competent and transforms it with a plasmid carrying the ampicillin resistance gene. By mistake, all the cells are spread on nutrient agar without any ampicillin. What is the most likely outcome?","image":"","options":["No colonies grow, because heat-shocked cells cannot divide without ampicillin","Only transformed cells form colonies, since competence kills untransformed cells","Only untransformed cells grow, because the plasmid is lost without antibiotic","Both transformed and untransformed cells form colonies, so transformants cannot be identified"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key idea: the ampR gene is a selectable marker. It only helps when ampicillin is in the medium.\nWithout ampicillin there is no selection pressure. Untransformed E. coli grow normally, and so do the plasmid-carrying cells.\nSo both types form colonies, and they cannot be told apart.\nOption (1) is wrong: heat-shocked cells divide normally and do not need ampicillin.\nOption (2) is wrong: competence (Ca$^{2+}$ treatment) does not kill untransformed cells.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The origin of replication in a vector also controls the copy number of the DNA linked to it","In the first artificial recombinant DNA, an antibiotic resistance gene was linked to a plasmid of E. coli, and the recombinant DNA was then transferred into Salmonella typhimurium","In a continuous culture system, used medium is drained out while fresh medium is added, keeping cells in the log/exponential phase","In pBR322, the rop gene codes for proteins involved in replication of the plasmid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the ori is where replication starts, and it also controls the copy number of the linked DNA.\n(2) Incorrect: Cohen and Boyer linked an antibiotic resistance gene to a native plasmid of Salmonella typhimurium. The recombinant DNA was then transferred into E. coli. The option reverses the two organisms.\n(3) Correct: continuous culture keeps cells in the log/exponential phase.\n(4) Correct: in pBR322, rop codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a cloning experiment, the foreign DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the β-galactosidase gene of the vector. The transformed bacteria are plated on a medium containing a chromogenic substrate. The colonies that show no colour are","image":"","options":["colonies whose plasmid carries no insert","colonies with an intact, active β-galactosidase gene","colonies carrying recombinant plasmids, with the β-galactosidase gene inactivated by the insert","colonies whose plasmid has lost its origin of replication"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Inserting foreign DNA within the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence inactivates the gene. This is called insertional inactivation.\nWithout the enzyme, the chromogenic substrate is not converted, so these recombinant colonies stay colourless.\nOptions (1) and (2) describe plasmids with no insert. Such plasmids keep an active enzyme and give blue colonies.\nOption (4) is wrong because a plasmid without an origin of replication cannot be maintained, so no colony would form at all.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"During agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments move through the gel towards which electrode, and why?","image":"","options":["Towards the cathode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is negatively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the cathode, because DNA is negatively charged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The phosphate groups in its backbone give DNA a negative charge.\nIn an electric field, opposite charges attract, so negatively charged DNA moves towards the positive electrode, the anode.\nOption (4) states the correct charge but sends DNA to the negative cathode, which would repel it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Genetic engineering\nB. Bioprocess engineering\nC. Sexual reproduction\nD. Asexual reproduction\nList-II\n(i) Preserves the genetic information\n(ii) Alters the chemistry of genetic material and changes the host phenotype\n(iii) Permits variation and unique combinations of genetic set-up\n(iv) Sterile ambience for large-scale growth of only the desired cells\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A, genetic engineering → (ii): it alters DNA or RNA chemistry and changes the host phenotype.\nB, bioprocess engineering → (iv): it keeps a sterile ambience so only the desired cells grow on a large scale.\nC, sexual reproduction → (iii): it permits variation and unique genetic combinations.\nD, asexual reproduction → (i): it preserves genetic information.\nThe common trap is swapping C and D, as in option (3).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A breeder crosses a high-yielding wheat variety with a wild relative to obtain a disease-resistance trait. The progeny become disease resistant but also inherit genes for poor grain quality from the wild parent. Which approach would best avoid this problem, and why?","image":"","options":["Propagate the hybrid asexually, because asexual reproduction preserves genetic information","Grow the hybrid in a sterile bioreactor, because bioprocess engineering allows only the desired cells to grow","Use genetic engineering to isolate and transfer only the resistance gene, because it avoids introducing undesirable genes","Perform more rounds of sexual crossing, because sexual reproduction creates new gene combinations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The problem is linkage drag. Crossing transfers the useful resistance gene together with unwanted genes from the wild parent.\nGenetic engineering uses recombinant DNA, gene cloning and gene transfer to isolate and introduce only the desired gene. Undesirable genes are not carried along.\n(1) fails: asexual propagation keeps both the good and the bad genes.\n(2) fails: bioprocess engineering grows cells on a large scale but does not select genes.\n(4) fails: more crossing only reshuffles genes at random.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the restriction enzyme EcoRI, the letters 'Eco' come from the genus and species of the bacterium, while 'R' comes from the strain.\nStatement II: The Roman numeral at the end of a restriction enzyme's name shows the order in which the enzyme was isolated from that strain of bacteria.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. EcoRI comes from *Escherichia coli* RY 13. 'E' is from the genus, 'co' from the species and 'R' from the strain.\nStatement II: Correct. The Roman numeral gives the order in which the enzyme was isolated from that strain. It does not give the number of recognition sites.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following components are required in a PCR reaction to amplify a gene of interest?\nTemplate (genomic) DNA, two primers, deoxyribonucleotides, thermostable DNA polymerase, DNA ligase, restriction endonuclease","image":"","options":["Three","Five","Six","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Required: template DNA. It contains the gene to be copied.\nRequired: two primers. They provide the starting points for synthesis.\nRequired: deoxyribonucleotides. They are the building blocks of the new strands.\nRequired: thermostable DNA polymerase. It extends the primers and survives the heating steps.\nNot required: DNA ligase and restriction endonuclease. These are used later to cut the amplified fragment and join it into a vector during cloning, not for amplification.\nSo four of the listed items are needed.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about restriction enzyme action at palindromic sites is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The enzyme cuts both strands between the same two bases on the opposite strands","The enzyme cuts exactly at the centre of the palindrome, so no single-stranded region remains","The single-stranded overhangs produced facilitate the action of DNA ligase","Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the enzyme cuts each strand between the same two bases.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT says the enzyme cuts a little away from the centre of the palindrome. This off-centre cut is what leaves single-stranded overhangs.\n(3) Correct: sticky overhangs facilitate the action of DNA ligase.\n(4) Correct: each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic sequence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A thermostable DNA polymerase makes it possible to carry out many repeated cycles of amplification in PCR.\nReason (R): The thermostable DNA polymerase remains active during the high-temperature denaturation of double-stranded DNA.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a thermostable polymerase lets PCR run many repeated cycles without stopping.\nR is true: Taq polymerase stays active through the high-temperature denaturation step.\nEvery cycle starts with heat denaturation. An ordinary polymerase would be inactivated in the first cycle, so the enzyme's survival at high temperature is exactly what allows repeated cycling.\nThus R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A figure shows an agarose gel after electrophoresis. Lane 1 contains undigested DNA and lanes 2 to 4 contain restriction-digested DNA. All bands lie between the wells at one end and the opposite end of the gel. The sample must have been loaded at the end connected to the:","image":"","options":["Anode, since DNA moves away from the positive electrode","Cathode, since DNA moves away from it towards the anode","Middle of the gel, so that fragments can move in both directions","Anode, since smaller fragments stay close to the well"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"DNA carries a negative charge because of its phosphate backbone. In an electric field it therefore moves towards the anode (positive electrode).\nThe bands lie between the wells and the far end of the gel. So the samples must have been loaded at the cathode (negative) end, and the DNA moved away from it towards the anode.\nOption (1) is wrong because DNA moves towards the anode, not away from it. Option (4) is wrong because smaller fragments move farthest from the well.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A small piece of foreign DNA is transferred into a host organism. It is not integrated into the host genome and is not linked to any replicating sequence. What is its most likely fate?","image":"","options":["It replicates on its own and is passed to all progeny cells","It will most likely not be able to multiply in the progeny cells of the host","It is converted into a plasmid by the host's DNA ligase","It makes the host cells resistant to antibiotics"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: alien DNA multiplies only if it becomes part of a chromosome or is linked to an origin of replication (ori). The ori is the sequence where replication starts.\nA free fragment that is neither integrated nor linked to an ori is not replicated. It is therefore lost as the host cells divide.\n(1) fails: a fragment cannot replicate on its own without an ori.\n(3) fails: ligase only joins DNA ends; it cannot make a plasmid.\n(4) fails: nothing in the question gives an antibiotic-resistance gene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The step in which separated DNA bands are cut out of the agarose gel and the DNA is extracted from the gel pieces is called:","image":"","options":["Spooling","Elution","Ligation","Annealing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cutting the separated bands out of the agarose gel and extracting the DNA from the gel pieces is called elution.\nSpooling is wrong: it is collecting precipitated DNA after ethanol is added.\nLigation is wrong: it is joining DNA ends.\nAnnealing is wrong: it is the step in PCR where primers bind to the template.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Making curd, bread or wine can be considered biotechnology in a broad sense. In the restricted sense used today, however, the term biotechnology mainly refers to processes that:","image":"","options":["Use naturally occurring microbes only in household fermentation","Use genetically modified organisms to achieve such processes on a larger scale","Use only chemical catalysts without any living material","Use only animals for producing food"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a broad sense, any use of microbes, such as making curd, bread or wine, is biotechnology.\nIn the restricted modern sense, biotechnology refers to processes that use genetically modified organisms to carry out such production on a larger scale.\nOption (1) describes only the traditional sense. Options (3) and (4) wrongly exclude microbes or GMOs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: By repeating the PCR cycle many times, a DNA segment can be amplified approximately a billion times.\nStatement II: PCR amplification takes place inside living E. coli cells using the host's own DNA polymerase.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: each cycle roughly doubles the target DNA. Repeating the cycle many times amplifies the segment about a billion times.\nStatement II is incorrect: PCR is an in vitro technique. It uses an added thermostable (Taq) polymerase, not living *E. coli* cells or their own polymerase.\nCopying DNA inside host cells describes cloning in a host, not PCR.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about agarose gel electrophoresis are correct?\n(a) DNA fragments migrate towards the cathode.\n(b) Agarose is a natural polymer obtained from seaweeds.\n(c) Larger DNA fragments travel farther from the well than smaller ones.\n(d) Ethidium bromide-stained DNA appears as bright orange bands under UV light.\n(e) Cutting out bands and extracting DNA from the gel pieces is called elution.\n(f) Agarose gel electrophoresis can be used to check the progress of a restriction enzyme digestion.","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Incorrect: negatively charged DNA moves towards the anode, not the cathode.\n(b) Correct: agarose is a natural polymer obtained from seaweeds.\n(c) Incorrect: smaller fragments travel farther, not larger ones.\n(d) Correct: EtBr-stained DNA shows bright orange bands under UV light.\n(e) Correct: extracting DNA from cut-out gel pieces is called elution.\n(f) Correct: the gel shows whether a restriction digestion has worked.\nFour statements are correct: (b), (d), (e) and (f).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the correct sequence of steps in a single cycle of PCR?","image":"","options":["Primer annealing → Denaturation → Extension","Extension → Primer annealing → Denaturation","Denaturation → Extension → Primer annealing","Denaturation → Primer annealing → Extension"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1, Denaturation: heat separates the double-stranded DNA into single strands.\nStep 2, Annealing: on cooling, the primers bind to their complementary sequences on the single strands.\nStep 3, Extension: DNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the primers, copying the template.\nWhy not (1): primers cannot anneal while the DNA is still double-stranded, so denaturation must come first.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Antibiotics, vaccines and enzymes are examples of products manufactured using bioprocess engineering","Genetic engineering introduces altered genetic material into a host organism, thereby changing its phenotype","Genetic engineering involves techniques that alter the chemistry of DNA only; RNA is never the target of such alteration","Traditional hybridisation cannot by itself prevent undesirable genes from being transferred along with desired ones"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: bioprocess engineering is used to make antibiotics, vaccines and enzymes on a large scale.\n(2) Correct: introducing altered genetic material into a host changes its phenotype.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT defines genetic engineering as techniques that alter the chemistry of genetic material, which means both DNA and RNA. Saying RNA is never a target is wrong.\n(4) Correct: traditional hybridisation often carries undesirable genes along with the desired ones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a continuous culture system, used medium is drained from one side and fresh medium is added from the other. In which phase are the cells kept by this arrangement?","image":"","options":["Lag phase","Log/exponential phase","Stationary phase","Decline phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a continuous culture, used medium is drained out from one side while fresh medium is added from the other.\nThis prevents nutrients from running out and waste from building up, so the cells stay in the log/exponential phase.\nIn this phase cells are physiologically most active, which gives more biomass and a higher yield of the desired protein.\nThe stationary phase is the tempting wrong choice. Cells reach it in a batch culture, which is exactly what continuous culture prevents.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Restriction endonuclease\nB. DNA ligase\nC. Sticky end\nD. Palindromic sequence\nList-II\n(i) Single-stranded overhang that hydrogen bonds with a complementary cut end\n(ii) Base-pair sequence that reads the same on both strands in the same orientation\n(iii) Cuts both strands in the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific points\n(iv) Joins the ends of cut DNA molecules\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–(iii): A restriction endonuclease cuts both strands of the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific points.\nB–(iv): DNA ligase joins the ends of cut DNA molecules.\nC–(i): A sticky end is a single-stranded overhang that hydrogen bonds with a complementary cut end.\nD–(ii): A palindromic sequence reads the same on both strands in the same orientation.\nOption (2) is the common trap because it swaps the cutting enzyme and the joining enzyme.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Agarose\n(B) Ethidium bromide\n(C) UV radiation\n(D) Elution\nList-II\n(I) Exposure needed to see stained DNA bands\n(II) Extraction of DNA from excised gel pieces\n(III) Matrix that provides the sieving effect\n(IV) Compound used to stain DNA\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Agarose → (III): it is the matrix that gives the sieving effect.\n(B) Ethidium bromide → (IV): it is the stain that binds DNA.\n(C) UV radiation → (I): stained DNA is visible only under UV light.\n(D) Elution → (II): it means extracting DNA from excised gel pieces.\nDo not swap the stain (EtBr) with the radiation used to view it (UV).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following steps of recombinant DNA technology in the correct sequence:\nP. Ligation of the DNA fragment into a vector\nQ. Fragmentation of DNA by restriction endonucleases\nR. Isolation of DNA\nS. Transferring the recombinant DNA into the host\nT. Isolation of the desired DNA fragment","image":"","options":["R → T → Q → P → S","Q → R → T → P → S","R → Q → T → P → S","R → Q → P → T → S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1 (R): Isolate DNA from the source cells.\nStep 2 (Q): Cut the DNA into fragments with restriction endonucleases.\nStep 3 (T): Isolate the desired fragment, for example by gel electrophoresis. This can only happen after cutting.\nStep 4 (P): Ligate the fragment into a vector cut with the same enzyme.\nStep 5 (S): Transfer the recombinant DNA into the host.\nOption (4) fails because it ligates before the desired fragment is isolated.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (method of gene transfer) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\nA. Micro-injection\nB. Biolistics\nC. Calcium treatment with heat shock\nD. Disarmed pathogen\nList-II:\nI. Used to make bacterial cells take up plasmid DNA\nII. Recombinant DNA delivered directly into the nucleus of an animal cell\nIII. DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles fired at cells; suited to plants\nIV. Vector infects the cell and transfers recombinant DNA into it","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Micro-injection matches II: DNA is injected directly into the nucleus of an animal cell.\nB. Biolistics matches III: DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles are fired at cells, a method suited to plants.\nC. Calcium treatment with heat shock matches I: it makes bacteria take up plasmid DNA.\nD. Disarmed pathogen matches IV: the vector infects the cell and transfers the recombinant DNA.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the polymerase chain reaction are correct?\n(a) Primers are small, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides complementary to regions of the target DNA.\n(b) A single primer is enough to amplify a DNA segment.\n(c) DNA polymerase extends the primers using the nucleotides provided in the reaction, with the genomic DNA as template.\n(d) PCR amplification takes place in vivo inside a host bacterium.\n(e) Repeated cycles can amplify a DNA segment about a billion times.\n(f) The DNA polymerase is destroyed in every cycle, so fresh enzyme must be added before each extension step.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: primers are small, chemically synthesised oligonucleotides complementary to regions of the target DNA.\n(b) Incorrect: two sets of primers are needed.\n(c) Correct: DNA polymerase extends the primers using the supplied nucleotides, with genomic DNA as the template.\n(d) Incorrect: PCR is carried out in vitro, not inside a host.\n(e) Correct: repeated cycles amplify a segment about a billion times.\n(f) Incorrect: thermostable Taq polymerase survives denaturation, so no fresh enzyme is needed each cycle.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease characterised whose action depended on a specific nucleotide sequence, and it recognises a sequence of six base pairs.\nStatement II: Restriction enzymes cut both DNA strands exactly at the centre of the palindromic site, and this is what produces sticky ends.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease characterised whose action depended on a specific sequence. It always recognises a specific six-base-pair sequence.\nStatement II is incorrect. Restriction enzymes cut a little away from the centre of the palindrome, between the same two bases on opposite strands. This leaves single-stranded overhangs called sticky ends. A cut exactly at the centre would give blunt ends instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes exonucleases from endonucleases?","image":"","options":["Exonucleases cut at specific positions within DNA, while endonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends","Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA, while endonucleases cut at specific positions within DNA","Exonucleases act only on RNA, while endonucleases act only on DNA","Exonucleases join DNA fragments, while endonucleases cut DNA fragments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Exonucleases and endonucleases are both nucleases, meaning they break DNA.\nExonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA.\nEndonucleases make cuts at specific positions inside the DNA molecule.\nOption (1) swaps the two roles.\nOption (4) is wrong because joining DNA fragments is done by DNA ligase, not by any nuclease.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign DNA fragment is ligated at the BamH I site of pBR322, and E. coli is transformed with the ligation mixture. Which statement correctly describes a recombinant colony?","image":"","options":["It grows on both ampicillin and tetracycline media","It grows on tetracycline medium but not on ampicillin medium","It grows on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium","It grows on neither ampicillin nor tetracycline medium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In pBR322 the BamH I site lies inside the tetracycline resistance gene ($tet^R$).\nInserting foreign DNA there disrupts $tet^R$ (insertional inactivation), but $amp^R$ stays intact.\nSo a recombinant colony survives ampicillin but is killed by tetracycline.\nOption (1) describes non-recombinant pBR322, which keeps both resistance genes intact.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"One strand of the EcoRI recognition site is 5'—GAATTC—3'. Its complementary strand, written in the 3' → 5' direction, is:","image":"","options":["3'—GAATTC—5'","3'—CTTAAG—5'","3'—CAATTG—5'","3'—GTTAAC—5'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pair each base of 5'—GAATTC—3' with its partner: G–C, A–T, A–T, T–A, T–A, C–G.\nThe complementary strand, written 3' → 5', is therefore CTTAAG.\nRead in the 5' → 3' direction, this strand is GAATTC, the same as the top strand. That is why the EcoRI site is a palindrome.\nOption (1) simply repeats the original strand and is not complementary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In pBR322, the Pst I site lies within the ampicillin resistance gene. A researcher inserts foreign DNA at the Pst I site and transforms E. coli. Which plating strategy correctly identifies the recombinants?","image":"","options":["Plate first on ampicillin, then transfer colonies to tetracycline; recombinants grow on ampicillin but not on tetracycline","Plate first on tetracycline, then transfer colonies to ampicillin; recombinants grow on tetracycline but not on ampicillin","Plate only on ampicillin; recombinants are the colonies that grow","Plate first on tetracycline, then transfer colonies to ampicillin; recombinants grow on both media"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Pst I site lies within $amp^R$, so an insert there inactivates ampicillin resistance while $tet^R$ still works.\nFirst plate on tetracycline. Every transformant (recombinant or not) grows, because $tet^R$ is intact.\nThen transfer the colonies to ampicillin. Recombinants fail to grow, since $amp^R$ is disrupted.\nOption (1) is the trap: it uses the BamH I/Sal I scheme, where the roles of the two antibiotics are reversed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In recombinant DNA technology, the abbreviation PCR stands for:","image":"","options":["Protein Chain Reaction","Polymerase Chain Reaction","Primer Cloning Reaction","Plasmid Copying Reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: PCR stands for Polymerase Chain Reaction.\nThe enzyme DNA polymerase copies a chosen DNA segment again and again in vitro. Each new copy becomes a template for the next cycle, so the copying builds into a 'chain' of reactions.\nWhy not (1): no protein is synthesised in PCR. Only DNA is amplified.\nOptions (3) and (4) are invented expansions and are not NCERT terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (enzyme used during DNA isolation) with List-II (what it acts on):\nList-I\nA. Lysozyme\nB. Cellulase\nC. Chitinase\nD. Protease\nList-II\nI. Fungal cell wall\nII. Proteins such as histones associated with DNA\nIII. Bacterial cell wall\nIV. Plant cell wall\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – Lysozyme breaks the bacterial cell wall → III.\nB – Cellulase breaks the plant cell wall → IV.\nC – Chitinase breaks the fungal cell wall, which contains chitin → I.\nD – Protease removes proteins such as histones bound to DNA → II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The thermostable DNA polymerase used for repeated amplification in PCR is isolated from:","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Thermus aquaticus","Salmonella typhimurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: PCR uses Taq polymerase, a heat-stable DNA polymerase isolated from the bacterium *Thermus aquaticus*.\nThis bacterium lives in hot springs, so its enzyme survives the roughly 94°C denaturation step of every cycle.\nWhy not (1): *E. coli* is the usual cloning host, but its polymerase is destroyed by high heat.\n*Agrobacterium tumefaciens* is a source of plant vectors (Ti plasmid). *Salmonella typhimurium* is mentioned in NCERT only as the source of the first recombinant DNA (with a plasmid antibiotic-resistance gene).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the action of EcoRI on its recognition site 5'-GAATTC-3' is incorrect?","image":"","options":["It cuts each of the two strands at a specific point in the sugar-phosphate backbone","It cuts exactly at the centre of the palindrome, producing ends with no single-stranded portions","It cuts between the same two bases on the opposite strands","The cut fragments carry single-stranded overhanging stretches at their ends"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: EcoRI cuts each strand at a specific point in the sugar-phosphate backbone.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT states that EcoRI cuts a little away from the centre of the palindrome. This leaves single-stranded overhangs, not ends without them.\n(3) Correct: It cuts between the same two bases, G and A, on both opposite strands.\n(4) Correct: The fragments carry single-stranded overhanging stretches called sticky ends.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Agarose, the most commonly used matrix in gel electrophoresis of DNA, is:","image":"","options":["A synthetic polymer made from acrylamide","A protein extracted from animal connective tissue","A natural polymer extracted from seaweeds","A polysaccharide extracted from fungal cell walls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes agarose as a natural polymer extracted from seaweeds.\nOption (1) is wrong because acrylamide is a synthetic material used for a different kind of gel.\nOption (2) is wrong because agarose is not a protein from connective tissue.\nOption (4) is wrong because agarose does not come from fungal cell walls.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence\n(B) Chromogenic substrate\n(C) T-DNA\n(D) Disarmed retrovirus\nList-II\n(I) Transforms normal plant cells into tumour cells\n(II) Site of insertion leading to insertional inactivation\n(III) Gene delivery vehicle for animal cells\n(IV) Produces blue colour with a functional enzyme","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Foreign DNA is inserted into the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence, which inactivates the gene (insertional inactivation): A-II.\n(B) The chromogenic substrate gives a blue colour only when the enzyme is functional: B-IV.\n(C) T-DNA from Agrobacterium tumefaciens turns normal plant cells into tumour cells: C-I.\n(D) A disarmed retrovirus has lost its ability to cause disease but still delivers genes, so it is a vehicle for animal cells: D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A restriction enzyme has exactly two recognition sites in a circular plasmid and exactly two recognition sites in a linear DNA molecule. Each DNA is completely digested and run in separate lanes of an agarose gel. Assuming all fragments differ in size, how many bands will appear in the plasmid lane and in the linear DNA lane, respectively?","image":"","options":["2 and 2","3 and 3","3 and 2","2 and 3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A circular molecule cut at $n$ sites gives $n$ fragments, because it has no free ends.\nA linear molecule cut at $n$ sites gives $n+1$ fragments, because the two ends form extra pieces.\nPlasmid (circular) with 2 sites gives 2 fragments, so 2 bands.\nLinear DNA with 2 sites gives $2+1 = 3$ fragments, so 3 bands.\nThe trap is assuming both molecules behave the same way.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In agarose gel electrophoresis, smaller DNA fragments are found farther away from the wells than larger fragments.\nReason (R): DNA fragments are negatively charged and therefore migrate towards the anode under an electric field.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: smaller fragments move farther from the wells than larger ones.\nR is true: DNA is negatively charged and moves towards the anode.\nR does not explain A. The charge only sets the direction in which DNA moves. Separation by size comes from the sieving effect of the agarose matrix, which lets smaller fragments move faster.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Small laboratory cultures cannot give appreciable amounts of a recombinant product, so bioreactors were developed. According to NCERT, these bioreactors can typically process culture volumes of about:","image":"","options":["1–10 litres","10–50 millilitres","100–1000 litres","10,000–1,00,000 litres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: shake-flask or small lab cultures give too little product. Bioreactors were developed to handle much larger culture volumes, about 100–1000 litres.\nWhy (3) fits: NCERT gives exactly this range as the scale needed for large-scale yields of recombinant products.\nWhy not (1) or (2): millilitres or a few litres are laboratory or shake-flask scales, not bioreactor scales.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"After the biosynthetic stage, the processes of separation and purification of the product are collectively referred to as:","image":"","options":["Upstream processing","Elution","Downstream processing","Insertional inactivation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): once the biosynthetic stage is complete, the product must be separated and purified before use. These steps together are called downstream processing.\nOption (3) fits because downstream processing covers exactly this separation and purification.\nElution is the tempting wrong option. It means recovering DNA bands cut out of an agarose gel, not purifying a final product.\nUpstream processing refers to steps before biosynthesis. Insertional inactivation is a method of selecting recombinants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher wants a foreign gene to be multiplied and passed on to the progeny of a bacterial host. Which approach is most consistent with the principle used by Cohen and Boyer?","image":"","options":["Join the gene to a plasmid that has an origin of replication, using cuts made by a restriction enzyme and joining by DNA ligase, then transfer it into the host","Introduce the naked gene fragment alone so that the host's DNA polymerase copies it directly","Remove the origin of replication from the plasmid before inserting the gene so the gene is not diluted","Treat the gene with DNA ligase alone so it circularises and replicates without any vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Alien DNA multiplies only when it is linked to an origin of replication (ori). A plasmid vector provides this ori.\nTo build the construct, a restriction enzyme cuts the gene and the vector, DNA ligase joins them, and the recombinant DNA is moved into the host.\nA naked fragment (2) or a self-ligated circle (4) has no ori, so it will not replicate. Removing the ori (3) also prevents replication.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the first recombinant DNA experiment (1972) are correct?\n(a) The native plasmid used belonged to Salmonella typhimurium.\n(b) The antibiotic resistance gene was cut out of a plasmid using restriction enzymes.\n(c) The recombinant DNA molecule was formed inside the E. coli cell.\n(d) In E. coli, the recombinant DNA replicated using the host's DNA polymerase.\n(e) The gene was joined to the plasmid by DNA polymerase.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the native plasmid came from Salmonella typhimurium.\n(b) Correct: restriction enzymes cut the antibiotic resistance gene out of a plasmid.\n(c) Incorrect: the recombinant DNA was made in vitro and only later transferred into E. coli.\n(d) Correct: in E. coli it replicated using the host's DNA polymerase.\n(e) Incorrect: DNA ligase joined the gene to the plasmid, not DNA polymerase.\nThree statements are correct: (a), (b) and (d).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The EFB definition of biotechnology covers only modern molecular biotechnology and excludes traditional microbe-based processes.\nStatement II: Genetic engineering and bioprocess engineering are the two core techniques that enabled the birth of modern biotechnology.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The EFB definition deliberately covers both the traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology.\nStatement II is correct. Genetic engineering and bioprocess engineering are the two core techniques behind modern biotechnology.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A DNA polymerase isolated from Thermus aquaticus is preferred for PCR.\nReason (R): This enzyme remains active during the high-temperature denaturation of double-stranded DNA, so the amplification cycles can be repeated.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Taq polymerase from *Thermus aquaticus* is the enzyme of choice for PCR.\nR is true: the enzyme stays active through the high-temperature denaturation step of each cycle.\nWhy R explains A: an ordinary polymerase would be denatured by this heat and would have to be added again every cycle. Because Taq survives the heat, the cycles can be repeated without adding fresh enzyme.\nSo both statements are true, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following double-stranded DNA sequences is a palindrome, that is, reads the same on both strands when each is read in the 5' → 3' direction? (Only the upper strand is shown.)","image":"","options":["5'-GGATTC-3'","5'-GGATCC-3'","5'-GAAGTC-3'","5'-AAGCCT-3'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands when each is read 5' → 3'. To test a sequence, write its complementary strand and read that strand 5' → 3'.\n(2) Correct: GGATCC pairs with 3'-CCTAGG-5'. Read 5' → 3', this is GGATCC, the same as the upper strand. It is the BamHI site.\n(1) Wrong: GGATTC gives 5'-GAATCC-3'.\n(3) Wrong: GAAGTC gives 5'-GACTTC-3'.\n(4) Wrong: AAGCCT gives 5'-AGGCTT-3'.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the E. coli cloning vector pBR322, what does the gene labelled 'rop' code for?","image":"","options":["Proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid","An enzyme that inactivates ampicillin","A restriction endonuclease that cuts at the BamH I site","A protein that pumps tetracycline out of the cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In pBR322, the rop gene codes for proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid.\nAmpicillin is inactivated by the product of ampR. Tetracycline resistance comes from tetR. So options (2) and (4) describe other genes.\nBamH I is a restriction site inside tetR, not a gene product, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are present in the E. coli cloning vector pBR322, as shown in the NCERT map?\nori, rop, ampR, tetR, T-DNA, kanamycin resistance gene","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Present in pBR322: ori (origin of replication), rop (replication proteins), ampR and tetR. That makes 4.\nT-DNA is absent: it is part of the Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens.\nThe kanamycin resistance gene is absent: it is a useful marker in general but is not on pBR322.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Thermus aquaticus\n(B) Agarose\n(C) Ethidium bromide\n(D) Chilled ethanol\nList-II\n(I) Visualisation of DNA bands under UV light\n(II) Precipitation of purified DNA\n(III) Source of thermostable DNA polymerase\n(IV) Natural polymer obtained from seaweeds\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Thermus aquaticus → (III): it is the source of the heat-stable Taq DNA polymerase used in PCR.\n(B) Agarose → (IV): it is a natural polymer extracted from seaweeds and is used as the gel matrix.\n(C) Ethidium bromide → (I): it stains DNA, which then shows as orange bands under UV light.\n(D) Chilled ethanol → (II): it precipitates purified DNA as fine threads.\nSo the correct matching is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Traditional hybridisation in plant and animal breeding often leads to the inclusion and multiplication of undesirable genes along with the desired genes.\nReason (R): Genetic engineering allows only one or a set of desirable genes to be isolated and introduced into a target organism.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Hybridisation transfers whole genomes, so undesirable genes are carried and multiplied along with the desired ones.\nR is true. Genetic engineering lets us isolate and introduce only one gene, or a chosen set of genes, into a target organism.\nHowever, R describes the advantage of genetic engineering over hybridisation. It does not explain why hybridisation carries unwanted genes, so R is not the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match each part of the enzyme name EcoRI in List-I with what it represents in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) E\n(B) co\n(C) R\n(D) I\nList-II\n(I) Strain of the bacterium\n(II) Order of isolation from that strain\n(III) Genus of the bacterium\n(IV) Species of the bacterium","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In EcoRI:\n(A) 'E' comes from the genus Escherichia, so A–III.\n(B) 'co' comes from the species coli, so B–IV.\n(C) 'R' comes from the strain RY 13, so C–I.\n(D) 'I' is the order of isolation from that strain, so D–II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which two antibiotic resistance genes are present in the cloning vector pBR322?","image":"","options":["Kanamycin and chloramphenicol resistance genes","Ampicillin and kanamycin resistance genes","Tetracycline and chloramphenicol resistance genes","Ampicillin and tetracycline resistance genes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"pBR322 carries two antibiotic resistance genes: ampR (ampicillin) and tetR (tetracycline).\nKanamycin and chloramphenicol resistance genes are also useful markers for E. coli in general. However, they are not part of pBR322, so options (1), (2) and (3) are wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a chromogenic substrate is used, bacterial colonies carrying a recombinant plasmid remain colourless.\nReason (R): The alien DNA is inserted into the rop gene, which codes for the enzyme that produces blue colour.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. With a chromogenic substrate, non-recombinant colonies turn blue. Recombinant colonies stay colourless because the insert sits inside the coding sequence of $\\beta$-galactosidase and inactivates it.\nR is false. The colour comes from $\\beta$-galactosidase, not from rop. The rop gene of pBR322 codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication.\nSo A is true but R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A cloning vector carries an ampicillin resistance gene and the coding sequence for β-galactosidase, with the cloning site lying inside the β-galactosidase gene. After a ligation reaction, E. coli cells are transformed and plated on a medium containing ampicillin and a chromogenic substrate. Which of the following inferences is correct?","image":"","options":["Blue colonies contain plasmids carrying the foreign DNA insert","Colourless colonies arise from transformed cells whose β-galactosidase gene has been inactivated by the insert","Untransformed E. coli cells form colourless colonies because they lack the plasmid-borne β-galactosidase gene","To distinguish recombinants, this method still requires plating on two plates containing different antibiotics"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only cells carrying the plasmid survive on ampicillin, because the plasmid supplies $amp^R$.\nIn these cells, an intact β-galactosidase gene acts on the chromogenic substrate and gives blue colonies. An insert inside this gene inactivates it, so recombinant colonies stay colourless. This makes (2) correct.\n(1) is wrong: blue colonies carry vectors without an insert.\n(3) is wrong: untransformed cells cannot grow on ampicillin at all.\n(4) is wrong: this colour-based screening was developed to replace the cumbersome two-plate antibiotic method.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the context of DNA, a sequence is called palindromic when it:","image":"","options":["reads the same on the two strands when both are read in the same orientation (e.g., 5' → 3')","reads the same forward and backward on a single strand, like the word MALAYALAM","contains only A and T bases arranged symmetrically","is repeated many times in tandem along the chromosome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: a DNA palindrome reads the same on both strands when the reading orientation is kept the same.\nExample: 5'-GAATTC-3' on one strand, and 5'-GAATTC-3' on the complementary strand also read 5'→3'.\nOption (2) is the tempting trap. A word palindrome like MALAYALAM reads one strand backward, which is not the DNA definition.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe base composition and tandem repeats, which are unrelated to palindromes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about introducing recombinant DNA into host cells are correct?\n(a) In biolistics, plant cells are bombarded with high-velocity gold or tungsten particles coated with DNA.\n(b) In micro-injection, recombinant DNA is injected directly into the cytoplasm of a plant cell.\n(c) Bacteria take up DNA after incubation with DNA on ice, a brief heat shock at $42^\\circ$C, and return to ice.\n(d) Bacterial cells are made competent by treating them with a monovalent cation such as sodium.\n(e) Disarmed retroviruses are used to deliver desirable genes into animal cells.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: in biolistics (gene gun), plant cells are bombarded with high-velocity gold or tungsten microparticles coated with DNA.\n(b) Incorrect: micro-injection puts recombinant DNA directly into the nucleus of an animal cell, not into a plant cell's cytoplasm.\n(c) Correct: the steps are ice, then heat shock at $42^\\circ$C, then back to ice. This lets the bacteria take up the DNA.\n(d) Incorrect: competence is induced by a divalent cation such as calcium ($Ca^{2+}$), not monovalent sodium.\n(e) Correct: disarmed (non-pathogenic) retroviruses are used as vectors to deliver genes into animal cells.\nSo three statements, (a), (c) and (e), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The overhanging single-stranded ends produced by EcoRI are called sticky ends because they can form hydrogen bonds with their complementary cut counterparts.\nStatement II: The stickiness of these ends hinders the action of DNA ligase.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. EcoRI leaves single-stranded overhangs. These form hydrogen bonds with complementary overhangs cut by the same enzyme, which is why they are called sticky ends.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says this stickiness facilitates the action of DNA ligase. Base pairing holds the two ends together so ligase can seal the backbone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"To monitor a restriction digestion, a student runs an agarose gel. Lane 1 has the undigested DNA, and lanes 2–4 have samples taken from the digestion reaction. After ethidium bromide staining and UV exposure, lane 1 shows a single band close to the wells, while lanes 2–4 show several bands spread further down the gel. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["The DNA in lanes 2–4 has not been cut, because cut DNA cannot be stained","The bands farthest from the wells are the largest fragments","The enzyme has cut the DNA into fragments, and the smaller fragments have moved farther from the wells","The single band in lane 1 is RNA left over because ribonuclease treatment was skipped"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA moves towards the anode. The gel acts as a sieve, separating fragments by size.\nSmaller fragments move farther from the wells. Larger ones stay nearer the wells.\nUndigested DNA is one large molecule, so it gives a single band near the wells (lane 1).\nThe several bands lower in lanes 2–4 show the enzyme has cut the DNA into smaller fragments.\nOption (2) reverses the size–distance relationship. Option (1) is wrong because cut DNA does stain with ethidium bromide.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When plated with a chromogenic substrate, bacterial colonies carrying a plasmid without an insert in the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene appear blue.\nReason (R): In such plasmids the $\\beta$-galactosidase coding sequence is intact, so the enzyme is produced and acts on the chromogenic substrate to give colour.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: colonies carrying a non-recombinant plasmid turn blue on a chromogenic substrate.\nR is true: no foreign DNA has been inserted, so the $\\beta$-galactosidase gene is intact and makes an active enzyme.\nR explains A: the enzyme acts on the chromogenic substrate to form a blue product.\nIn recombinants, the insert inactivates the gene (insertional inactivation), so those colonies stay colourless.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of pBR322) with List-II (its role/description).\nList-I:\nA. ori\nB. ampR\nC. rop\nD. BamH I\nList-II:\nI. Codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication\nII. Restriction site within the tetracycline resistance gene\nIII. Sequence where replication starts; controls copy number\nIV. Selectable marker conferring ampicillin resistance","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. ori is where replication starts, and it controls copy number: III.\nB. ampR is the selectable marker for ampicillin resistance: IV.\nC. rop codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication: I.\nD. BamH I is a restriction site lying within tetR: II.\nDo not swap ori and rop. ori is a DNA sequence, while rop is a gene that codes for proteins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the first construction of an artificial recombinant DNA molecule by Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer in 1972, the antibiotic resistance gene was linked with a native plasmid of which organism?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Salmonella typhimurium","Agrobacterium tumifaciens","Thermus aquaticus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in 1972 Cohen and Boyer linked an antibiotic resistance gene to a native plasmid of Salmonella typhimurium.\nE. coli is the tempting choice, but it was only the host into which the recombinant plasmid was later transferred.\nAgrobacterium tumefaciens is a vector used for plants, and Thermus aquaticus is the source of Taq polymerase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Stirrer of a stirred-tank bioreactor\nB. Sparger\nC. Sampling port\nD. Downstream processing\nList-II\nI. Periodic withdrawal of small volumes of culture\nII. Separation and purification of the product\nIII. Even mixing and oxygen availability throughout the vessel\nIV. Bubbling sterile air through the culture\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – Stirrer: mixes the contents evenly so oxygen is available throughout the vessel → III.\nB – Sparger: bubbles sterile air into the culture → IV.\nC – Sampling port: lets small volumes of culture be withdrawn periodically → I.\nD – Downstream processing: separation and purification of the product → II.\nAvoid swapping the stirrer (mixing) with the sparger (air delivery).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Before transformation, bacterial cells are treated with a specific concentration of calcium ions.\nReason (R): DNA is a hydrophilic molecule and cannot pass through cell membranes on its own.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Bacterial cells are treated with a specific concentration of a divalent cation such as $Ca^{2+}$ to make them 'competent'.\nR is true. DNA is hydrophilic and cannot pass through cell membranes on its own.\nR explains A. Calcium treatment increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in the cell wall.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"When a restriction endonuclease cuts DNA at its recognition site, the bonds it breaks are located in the:","image":"","options":["hydrogen bonds between complementary bases","sugar-phosphate backbones of both strands","glycosidic bonds linking bases to sugars","peptide bonds of histone proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: a restriction endonuclease first binds its specific recognition sequence.\nIt then cuts each of the two strands at specific points in the sugar-phosphate backbone, breaking phosphodiester bonds.\nOption (1) is the tempting one, but hydrogen bonds hold the two strands together and are not what the enzyme cuts.\nGlycosidic bonds (3) are not cleaved by this enzyme. Peptide bonds (4) are in proteins, not DNA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A continuous culture system gives a higher yield of the desired recombinant protein than a simple small-scale culture.\nReason (R): In a continuous culture, the used medium is drained while fresh medium is added, so cells remain in their log phase and produce more biomass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that a continuous culture system gives a larger biomass, and therefore a higher yield of the desired protein.\nR is true. In continuous culture, used medium is drained out while fresh medium is added. This keeps the cells in their physiologically most active log (exponential) phase.\nR explains A. Cells kept in the log phase produce more biomass, and more biomass means more protein.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In agarose gel electrophoresis, larger DNA fragments travel farther from the well than smaller fragments.\nStatement II: DNA bands in a gel can be seen as bright orange bands only after staining with ethidium bromide and exposing the gel to UV light.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The agarose matrix acts as a sieve, so smaller fragments move farther from the well than larger ones.\nStatement II is correct. Pure DNA is invisible in visible light. It shows up as bright orange bands only after staining with ethidium bromide and exposing the gel to UV light.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (step in DNA isolation and cutting) with List-II (agent or technique used).\nList-I\nA. Removal of histones associated with DNA\nB. Removal of RNA from the cell extract\nC. Precipitation of purified DNA as fine threads\nD. Checking the progress of a restriction digestion\nList-II\nI. Chilled ethanol\nII. Agarose gel electrophoresis\nIII. Protease\nIV. Ribonuclease","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Histones are proteins and are removed by protease, while RNA is removed by ribonuclease. Purified DNA comes out of solution as threads when chilled ethanol is added, and these threads can be spooled. Agarose gel electrophoresis is used to monitor how far a restriction digestion has progressed. The key trap is swapping protease and ribonuclease.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A PCR is started with a single double-stranded DNA molecule containing the target gene. Assume every cycle doubles the number of target DNA molecules. Approximately how many copies will be present after 30 cycles?","image":"","options":["60","900","About $10^{9}$","About $10^{30}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 1 target DNA molecule, and each cycle doubles the number.\nFormula: copies after $n$ cycles $= 2^{n}$.\nSubstituting: $2^{30} = 1{,}073{,}741{,}824 \\approx 1.07 \\times 10^{9}$.\nSo about $10^{9}$ (a billion) copies are made, matching NCERT's figure. The value 60 assumes linear addition ($2 \\times 30$), and $10^{30}$ wrongly uses base 10 instead of base 2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In a continuous culture system, used medium is drained out from one side while fresh medium is added from the other. This keeps the cells in which growth phase?","image":"","options":["Lag phase","Log (exponential) phase","Stationary phase","Decline (death) phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a continuous culture, used medium is drained out from one side while fresh medium is added from the other.\nThis prevents nutrients from running out and waste from building up.\nAs a result, cells stay in the log (exponential) phase, when they are physiologically most active and give more biomass or product.\nThe tempting option, the stationary phase, is reached in a closed (batch) culture once nutrients are used up.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When DNA is isolated from animal tissue, the lysate is treated with protease.\nReason (R): Genes lie on long DNA molecules that are intertwined with proteins such as histones.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Animal cell lysate is treated with protease during DNA isolation.\nR is true. DNA is wound around histones and associated with other proteins.\nThese proteins must be digested to get pure DNA that restriction enzymes can cut. So R directly explains why protease is used in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Year in which two phage-restricting enzymes of E. coli were isolated\nB. Length of the recognition sequence of Hind II (in base pairs)\nC. Number of restriction enzymes known today (more than)\nD. Number of bacterial strains from which restriction enzymes have been isolated (over)\nList-II\n(i) 6\n(ii) 230\n(iii) 900\n(iv) 1963\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The two phage-restricting enzymes were isolated in 1963, so A-(iv).\nB: Hind II recognises a specific 6 bp sequence, so B-(i).\nC: More than 900 restriction enzymes are known today, so C-(iii).\nD: These enzymes come from over 230 bacterial strains, so D-(ii).\nTrap: option (2) swaps the 900 and 230 figures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, bioreactors were developed so that large volumes of culture could be processed. What is the typical volume range of such bioreactors?","image":"","options":["1–10 litres","10–50 litres","100–1000 litres","10,000–100,000 litres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Small laboratory flask cultures cannot give appreciable amounts of product.\nTo scale up, NCERT describes bioreactors that can process large culture volumes of 100–1000 litres.\nRanges of 1–10 or 10–50 litres are too small for this purpose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher finds a nuclease that steadily degrades linear DNA fragments but leaves an intact, covalently closed circular plasmid completely undigested. What is the most appropriate classification of this nuclease?","image":"","options":["An endonuclease, because it cuts at specific positions within the DNA","A restriction endonuclease, because it recognises a palindromic sequence","An exonuclease, because it needs free DNA ends to remove nucleotides","A DNA ligase, because it acts only on cut DNA molecules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: exonucleases remove nucleotides from the free ends of DNA. Endonucleases cut at specific positions within the DNA.\nLinear fragments have free 5' and 3' ends, so this enzyme can degrade them steadily.\nA covalently closed circular plasmid has no free ends, so an end-dependent enzyme cannot act on it. That is why it stays intact.\nAn endonuclease (options 1 and 2) cuts internally, so it could cut a circular plasmid if its site were present. Ligase (4) joins DNA and does not degrade it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In which method of gene transfer is recombinant DNA directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell?","image":"","options":["Biolistics","Heat shock transformation","Disarmed pathogen vector","Micro-injection"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: In micro-injection, recombinant DNA is injected directly into the nucleus of an animal cell.\nBiolistics, option (1), fires DNA-coated gold or tungsten particles and is suited to plants. It does not inject DNA into the nucleus.\nHeat shock, option (2), is used to transform competent bacteria.\nDisarmed pathogen vectors, option (3), deliver DNA by infecting the host cell, not by injection.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the correct sequence of steps for forcing recombinant DNA into competent bacterial cells?","image":"","options":["Incubate cells with recombinant DNA at 42°C → place on ice → return to 42°C","Treat cells with Ca$^{2+}$ → incubate with recombinant DNA on ice → brief exposure to 42°C → back on ice","Incubate cells with recombinant DNA on ice → treat with Ca$^{2+}$ at 42°C for a long period → keep at room temperature","Brief exposure to 42°C → treat with Ca$^{2+}$ → incubate with recombinant DNA on ice"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Treat the cells with Ca$^{2+}$ to make them competent.\nStep 2: Incubate the competent cells with recombinant DNA on ice.\nStep 3: Give a brief heat shock at 42°C.\nStep 4: Return the cells to ice.\nOption (3) is wrong because it adds Ca$^{2+}$ too late and makes the heat exposure prolonged.\nOptions (1) and (4) skip the competence step or place it in the wrong order.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign DNA fragment is ligated at the BamH I site of pBR322, and the recombinant plasmid is introduced into E. coli. What growth pattern will the bacteria carrying this recombinant plasmid show?","image":"","options":["Growth on both ampicillin and tetracycline media","Growth on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium","Growth on tetracycline medium but not on ampicillin medium","No growth on either ampicillin or tetracycline medium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The BamH I site of pBR322 lies within the tetracycline resistance gene (tetR).\nInserting foreign DNA there inactivates tetR (insertional inactivation), while ampR remains intact.\nSo recombinants grow on ampicillin medium but not on tetracycline medium.\nGrowth on both media (option 1) is the pattern of non-recombinant transformants.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher wants to isolate DNA from a fungus. Which enzyme should be used first to break open the cells?","image":"","options":["Lysozyme","Cellulase","Chitinase","Ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Before DNA can be isolated, the cell wall must be broken down by the appropriate enzyme.\nFungal cell walls are made of chitin, so chitinase is used.\nLysozyme is used for bacterial cells, and cellulase is used for plant cells.\nRibonuclease is used only after the cells have been opened, to remove RNA. It cannot break open cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"To isolate DNA from a fungal mycelium, the cell wall must first be broken. Which enzyme is most suitable for this step?","image":"","options":["Lysozyme","Cellulase","Chitinase","Ribonuclease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fungal cell walls are made mainly of chitin, so chitinase is used to break them open.\nLysozyme is used for bacterial cells, and cellulase is used for plant cells.\nRibonuclease only removes RNA after the cell has already been lysed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Genes for resistance to ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin are useful selectable markers for E. coli mainly because:","image":"","options":["these antibiotics are produced by E. coli itself","these genes increase the copy number of the plasmid","normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against these antibiotics","these genes contain multiple restriction sites for cloning"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A selectable marker must separate transformants from non-transformants.\nNormal E. coli cells have no resistance to ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin.\nOnly cells that took up a vector carrying the resistance gene survive on the antibiotic medium. Non-transformants die.\nThese genes do not raise copy number, which is the role of ori, and E. coli does not produce these antibiotics.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Herbert Boyer\n(B) Stanley Cohen\n(C) Escherichia coli\n(D) Origin of replication\nList-II:\n(I) Host in which the first recombinant plasmid was multiplied\n(II) Restriction enzymes producing sticky ends\n(III) Sequence responsible for initiating replication\n(IV) Method of removing plasmids from cells and reinserting them into other cells","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Herbert Boyer worked on restriction enzymes that make sticky ends → II.\n(B) Stanley Cohen developed the method of removing plasmids from cells and putting them into other cells → IV.\n(C) Escherichia coli was the host in which the recombinant plasmid was multiplied → I.\n(D) The origin of replication is the sequence where replication starts → III.\nThe correct match is A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The progress of a restriction enzyme digestion is checked by agarose gel electrophoresis. Towards which electrode do the DNA fragments move, and why?","image":"","options":["Towards the cathode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is negatively charged","Towards the anode, because DNA is positively charged","Towards the cathode, because DNA is negatively charged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the phosphate groups in the DNA backbone carry a negative charge.\nOpposite charges attract, so in an electric field DNA moves towards the positive electrode, the anode.\nOptions (1) and (3) are wrong because DNA is not positively charged.\nOption (4) is wrong because a negative molecule cannot move towards the negative cathode.\nIn the gel, smaller fragments travel farther through the agarose matrix.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base so that its contents mix properly.\nStatement II: In a sparged stirred-tank bioreactor, sterile air bubbles are passed through the culture.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Stirred-tank bioreactors are usually cylindrical or have a curved base so that the contents mix well.\nStatement II is correct. In a sparged stirred-tank bioreactor, sterile air bubbles are passed through the culture. This raises oxygen transfer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"In the name of the restriction enzyme EcoRI, what does the letter 'R' stand for?","image":"","options":["The genus of the bacterium","The strain of the bacterium","The order in which the enzyme was isolated","The recognition sequence of the enzyme"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"EcoRI comes from Escherichia coli RY 13.\nE stands for the genus (Escherichia) and 'co' for the species (coli).\nR stands for the strain, RY 13, which matches option (2).\nThe Roman numeral I gives the order in which the enzyme was isolated, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A company grows recombinant E. coli in a closed vessel with a fixed amount of medium. Product output per run levels off because the cells stop growing actively as nutrients are used up and wastes build up. Which change, based on NCERT, would most directly increase the yield of the recombinant protein?","image":"","options":["Adding a second selectable marker gene to the plasmid","Switching to continuous culture, draining spent medium while adding fresh medium","Increasing the frequency of withdrawal through sampling ports","Beginning downstream processing before the culture reaches its peak density"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): in a closed (batch) culture, nutrients run out and wastes accumulate, so cells leave the active growth phase.\nContinuous culture drains spent medium and adds fresh medium. This keeps cells in the log phase, giving more biomass and a higher protein yield, so option (2) fits.\nOption (1) fails because a second selectable marker only helps identify transformants. It does not affect growth.\nOption (3) fails because sampling ports remove only small volumes for monitoring.\nOption (4) fails because starting downstream processing early would lower the yield.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When foreign DNA is ligated at the BamH I site of the cloning vector pBR322, the transformed recombinant E. coli cells grow on an ampicillin medium but fail to grow on a tetracycline medium.\nReason (R): The BamH I site lies within the tetracycline resistance gene, and inserting foreign DNA there inactivates that gene.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In pBR322, the BamH I site lies inside the tetracycline resistance gene ($tet^R$). The ampicillin resistance gene ($amp^R$) is untouched, so recombinant cells still grow on ampicillin.\nR is true. A foreign DNA insert at the BamH I site disrupts $tet^R$. This is called insertional inactivation, and it makes recombinants sensitive to tetracycline.\nR directly explains A: losing $tet^R$ is why the recombinants fail on tetracycline but survive on ampicillin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): To create a recombinant vector, the vector DNA and the source DNA are normally cut with the same restriction enzyme.\nReason (R): DNA fragments cut by the same restriction enzyme carry the same kind of sticky ends, which can be joined end-to-end by DNA ligase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The vector and the source DNA are cut with the same restriction enzyme when making a recombinant vector.\nR is true. The same enzyme produces identical, complementary sticky ends on both molecules. These ends base pair and are then sealed by DNA ligase.\nR explains A: matching sticky ends are the very reason the same enzyme is used.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"To make E. coli cells 'competent' for taking up recombinant DNA, they are treated with a specific concentration of:","image":"","options":["A monovalent cation such as sodium","A divalent anion such as sulphate","A divalent cation such as calcium","The enzyme lysozyme to remove the cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: E. coli cells are made competent by treating them with a specific concentration of a divalent cation, such as calcium (Ca$^{2+}$).\nThis treatment increases how efficiently DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall.\nSodium is monovalent, and sulphate is an anion, so options (1) and (2) do not fit.\nLysozyme, option (4), digests the bacterial cell wall. It is used to break cells open during DNA isolation, not to make them competent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments are separated mainly on the basis of their size.\nStatement II: Pure DNA fragments in the gel can be seen directly under visible light without any staining.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The sieving effect of the agarose matrix separates DNA fragments mainly by size, and smaller fragments move farther.\nStatement II is incorrect. Pure DNA cannot be seen in visible light. It must first be stained with ethidium bromide and then exposed to UV light, where it shows bright orange bands.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The first artificial recombinant DNA molecule was constructed by linking an antibiotic resistance gene with a native plasmid of which organism?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Salmonella typhimurium","Thermus aquaticus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In 1972, Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer made the first recombinant DNA.\nThey linked an antibiotic resistance gene to a native plasmid of Salmonella typhimurium.\nE. coli is the tempting wrong option. It was the host into which the recombinant plasmid was later transferred, not the source of the plasmid.\nAgrobacterium is a vector used for plants, and Thermus aquaticus is the source of Taq polymerase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose three different restriction enzymes are isolated, one after another, from strain H of the bacterium Bacillus amyloliquefaciens. Following the standard naming convention, what would the third enzyme be called?","image":"","options":["BaHIII","BamHIII","BamIIIH","AmyBHIII"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The naming rule has four parts:\n1. Capital first letter of the genus: B (Bacillus).\n2. First two letters of the species: am (amyloliquefaciens).\n3. Letter for the strain: H.\n4. Roman numeral for the order of isolation: III for the third enzyme.\nPutting these together gives BamHIII.\nOption (1) uses only one species letter, option (3) puts the parts in the wrong order, and option (4) begins with species letters instead of the genus letter.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"The first restriction endonuclease whose action depended on a specific nucleotide sequence was Hind II. Its recognition sequence is made up of how many base pairs?","image":"","options":["Four","Five","Six","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says Hind II always cuts DNA at a particular point after recognising a specific sequence.\nThis recognition sequence is six base pairs long.\nFour-base-pair sites belong to some other enzymes. They are not the site NCERT gives for Hind II, so option (1) is a distractor.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which organism naturally delivers a piece of DNA called 'T-DNA' into the cells of several dicot plants and turns them into tumour cells?","image":"","options":["Escherichia coli","Thermus aquaticus","Agrobacterium tumefaciens","Salmonella typhimurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants. It transfers T-DNA from its Ti (tumour-inducing) plasmid into plant cells, transforming them into tumour cells.\nThermus aquaticus is the source of Taq polymerase used in PCR; it is not a plant pathogen.\nE. coli is the usual cloning host, and Salmonella typhimurium supplied a plasmid used in early rDNA work. Neither delivers T-DNA to plants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about large-scale production of a recombinant protein:\nA. In a continuous culture, used medium is drained from one side and fresh medium is added from the other, keeping cells in the log/exponential phase.\nB. A stirred-tank bioreactor is usually cylindrical or has a curved base to help mix its contents.\nC. In a sparged stirred-tank bioreactor, sterile air bubbles are passed through the culture.\nD. Downstream processing is the biosynthetic stage in which host cells synthesise the recombinant protein inside the bioreactor.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A, B and C only","A, C and D only","B and D only","A, B, C and D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is correct. Continuous culture drains used medium from one side and adds fresh medium from the other. This keeps cells in the active log/exponential phase.\nB is correct. Stirred-tank reactors are cylindrical or have a curved base to aid mixing.\nC is correct. In a sparged stirred-tank reactor, sterile air bubbles are passed through the culture.\nD is incorrect. Downstream processing comes after biosynthesis. It covers separation, purification, formulation and quality control.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A ligation mixture containing pBR322 cut at the BamH I site and joined with foreign DNA was used to transform E. coli. The cells were spread on an ampicillin plate, which gave 240 colonies. These colonies were replica-plated onto a tetracycline plate, where 180 colonies grew at matching positions. How many of the original colonies carry recombinant plasmids?","image":"","options":["60","180","240","420"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 240 colonies on ampicillin (all transformants) and 180 colonies on tetracycline.\nA BamH I insert lies in tetR, so recombinants lose tetracycline resistance through insertional inactivation.\nThe 180 colonies that grow on tetracycline have an intact tetR, so they are non-recombinants.\nRecombinants = $240 - 180 = 60$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Normally, a recombinant vector molecule cannot be created unless the vector and the source DNA are cut with the same restriction enzyme.\nStatement II: A restriction enzyme cuts both strands a little away from the centre of the palindrome but between the same two bases, so all fragments it produces carry complementary sticky ends that can pair and be sealed by DNA ligase.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A recombinant molecule normally forms only when the vector and the source DNA are cut with the same restriction enzyme, so their ends match.\nStatement II is correct. The enzyme cuts a little away from the centre of the palindrome, but between the same two bases on both strands. This leaves single-stranded overhangs (sticky ends). These ends pair with complementary ends by hydrogen bonds, and DNA ligase then seals them.\nStatement II explains why Statement I holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"According to present knowledge, roughly how many restriction enzymes have been isolated, and from how many bacterial strains?","image":"","options":["More than 230 enzymes from over 900 strains","More than 90 enzymes from over 23 strains","More than 900 enzymes from over 230 strains","More than 9000 enzymes from over 2300 strains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that more than 900 restriction enzymes have been isolated from over 230 strains of bacteria.\nEach of these enzymes recognises a different sequence.\nOption (1) is a trap because it swaps the two numbers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Why is selecting recombinants by inactivation of an antibiotic resistance gene considered a cumbersome procedure?","image":"","options":["It requires plating on two plates containing different antibiotics","It requires heat shock at $42^\\circ$C for every colony","It needs ethidium bromide staining of each colony","It works only with plant cells and not with bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In insertional inactivation of an antibiotic resistance gene, transformants must be grown on one antibiotic plate and then transferred to a second plate with another antibiotic (e.g., ampicillin, then tetracycline).\nThis simultaneous plating on two plates is laborious, which is why chromogenic selection was developed.\nHeat shock is part of transformation, not selection, and ethidium bromide is used to stain DNA in gels.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In both the antibiotic-based and the chromogenic methods, recombinants are recognised because the inserted DNA disrupts a gene present in the vector","Chromogenic selection distinguishes recombinants from non-recombinants without plating on two different antibiotic media","Retroviruses used as vectors for animal cells retain their ability to convert normal cells into cancerous cells","The idea of using Ti plasmids and retroviruses as vectors came from understanding how pathogens deliver genes to their eukaryotic hosts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: both methods rely on insertional inactivation of a vector gene (an antibiotic-resistance gene or $\\beta$-galactosidase).\n(2) Correct: chromogenic selection avoids the cumbersome step of replica plating on two antibiotic media.\n(3) Incorrect: retroviral vectors are disarmed. Their disease-causing ability is removed and only their gene-delivery capacity is kept. Natural retroviruses can transform cells, but the vectors cannot.\n(4) Correct: these vectors were developed from understanding how pathogens transfer genes to eukaryotic hosts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose a foreign gene is inserted into a restriction site lying within the ampR gene of pBR322, and the tetR gene is left intact. Which strategy would correctly separate recombinants from non-recombinants?","image":"","options":["Plate transformants on ampicillin; recombinants are the colonies that grow","Plate transformants on tetracycline, then replica-plate onto ampicillin; recombinants are colonies that fail to grow on ampicillin","Plate transformants on ampicillin, then replica-plate onto tetracycline; recombinants are colonies that fail to grow on tetracycline","Plate transformants on a medium with both antibiotics; recombinants are the colonies that grow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An insert in ampR inactivates ampicillin resistance (insertional inactivation), while tetR stays functional.\nPlating on tetracycline selects all transformants, both recombinant and non-recombinant.\nOn replica plating onto ampicillin, non-recombinants still grow because ampR is intact. Recombinants fail to grow because ampR is disrupted.\nOption (1) fails because recombinants are now ampicillin-sensitive, so they would not grow on ampicillin. This is the reverse of the NCERT BamH I (tetR) example.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"A foreign gene is ligated at the BamH I site of the vector pBR322, and the plasmids are used to transform E. coli. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Recombinant transformants grow on a medium containing ampicillin.\n(b) Recombinant transformants grow on a medium containing tetracycline.\n(c) Non-recombinant transformants grow on media containing either antibiotic.\n(d) Untransformed E. coli cells grow on a medium containing ampicillin.\n(e) The rop gene of pBR322 codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The BamH I site lies in the $tet^R$ gene, so inserting a gene there inactivates tetracycline resistance.\n(a) Correct: recombinants still have intact $amp^R$, so they grow on ampicillin.\n(b) Incorrect: $tet^R$ is disrupted, so recombinants cannot grow on tetracycline.\n(c) Correct: non-recombinants have both resistance genes and grow on either antibiotic.\n(d) Incorrect: untransformed E. coli has no ampicillin resistance.\n(e) Correct: the rop gene codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Selection using a chromogenic substrate avoids the cumbersome need to plate on two plates containing different antibiotics.\n(b) Blue colonies on a chromogenic substrate medium carry plasmids with the foreign DNA insert.\n(c) Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several monocot plants and transfers T-DNA into them.\n(d) The modified Ti plasmid is no longer pathogenic to plants but still uses the mechanism of Agrobacterium to deliver genes.\n(e) Retroviruses can naturally transform normal animal cells into cancerous cells, and disarmed retroviruses are used as vectors for animal cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: chromogenic selection avoids plating on two different antibiotic plates.\n(b) Incorrect: blue colonies have an intact $\\beta$-galactosidase gene, so their plasmids carry no insert.\n(c) Incorrect: A. tumefaciens is a pathogen of dicots, not monocots.\n(d) Correct: the modified Ti plasmid is non-pathogenic but still uses Agrobacterium's mechanism to deliver genes.\n(e) Correct: natural retroviruses can cause cancer, and disarmed retroviruses serve as vectors for animal cells.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-BIOTECH-PRINCIPLES-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Selection of recombinants using a chromogenic substrate is preferred over selection based on inactivation of antibiotic resistance genes.\nReason (R): Selection based on inactivation of antibiotic resistance requires plating on only a single plate containing one antibiotic.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: selection by insertional inactivation of antibiotic resistance genes is cumbersome. So the chromogenic (blue-white) method is preferred.\nR is false: antibiotic-based selection needs simultaneous plating on two plates, each containing a different antibiotic, not just one.\nIn chromogenic selection, the insert inactivates the β-galactosidase gene. Colonies with no insert turn blue. Recombinant colonies stay colourless, so they can be picked out on a single plate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 9 · Biotechnology: Principles and Processes","difficulty":"medium"}]});
